nium salt; pH 7-10; approximately 48 hours. No external reductant or metal catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate-derived imine/hemiaminal species undergo intermolecular hydride transfer. One carbon skeleton becomes glycine while another becomes oxamate; water completes the net balance.", "reasoning": "Jarois et al. (2024) measured approximately 11-14% glycine under concentrated buffered conditions and supported hydride transfer isotopically. Little reaction occurred below roughly 0.25 M glyoxylate on the experimental timescale, and unbuffered ammonium hydroxide did not reproduce the result. This therefore requires a concentrated pool or pore fluid, not a dilute ocean. Tested nonphosphate buffers avoid assuming unlimited phosphate availability in Fe2+-rich water."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrogen-driven nickel-catalyzed glyoxylate reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Exposed native nickel or Ni-bearing meteoritic metal surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300 K", "pressure": "approximately 5 bar H2", "catalyst": "reactive nickel-bearing metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8; representative ketoacid concentration around 30 mM; ammonium bicarbonate medium; approximately 72 hours. A cool nonenzymatic mineral interface is modeled, not a hot vent interior."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms a glyoxylate imine, and nickel activates molecular hydrogen for imine reduction. Competing carbonyl reduction can produce hydroxy-acid side products.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven ketoacid reduction and glycine formation with nickel-containing catalysts, including meteoritic material. H2 is the terminal reductant; nickel is not substituted for the reducing-equivalent inventory. Serpentinization provides a plausible H2 source, but sufficiently reactive natural Ni surfaces, catalyst poisoning and glyoxylate supply remain unresol