y active nickel material and justify its availability, persistence and accessibility in the proposed setting.\",\n        \"Specify hydrogen partial pressure, pH, ammonium concentration, temperature and residence time, using the reported approximately 5 bar H2 and room-temperature conditions as a benchmark.\",\n        \"Quantify competing glyoxylate reduction to hydroxy-acid products and potential catalyst poisoning.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.61,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"The explicit oxidation of Fe0 to Fe(II) correctly accounts for the reducing equivalents and avoids treating the reductant as an indefinitely reusable catalyst. Kitadai et al. (2019) supports glyoxylate reductive amination by electrochemically pre-reduced FeS/Fe0 assemblages. The unresolved environmental issue is obtaining and maintaining comparably reactive material naturally. With two proton equivalents, the equation is balanced. Its formal H+ bookkeeping is not evidence that acidic bulk water is required; alkaline protonation and iron speciation still need definition.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify how the active FeS/Fe0 assemblage forms and persists, rather than assuming ordinary FeS has the same activity.\",\n        \"Provide pH, temperature, substrate concentrations and reactive-metal loading, together with Fe(II) precipitation or complexation behavior.\",\n        \"Store the two-H+ coefficient explicitly while distinguishing formal stoichiometry from actual solution speciation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.68,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.48,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.54,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.63,\n      \"feedback\": \"N-oxalylglycine formation agrees with Yanagawa et al. (1984), and rxn_012 balances with two glyoxylic acid equivalents. The central limitation is liberation of free glycine: the cited yields followed approximately 6 M HCl hydrolysis, not demonstrated release under the precursor-forming aqueous conditions. Amide hydrolysis is chemically legitimate, but its environmental rate and selectivity are unestablished here. The network appropriately labels rxn_013 conditional; that caveat prevents overclaiming but does not validate target release.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the acidic precursor-formation regime, including the reported pH 3\u20134 optimum, and record rxn_012 coefficients.\",\n        \"Measure N-oxalylglycine hydrolysis under physically justified environmental conditions, including glycine survival.\",\n        \"Until release is demonstrated, distinguish this branch's supported reservoir production from conditional free-glycine production.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.76,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.68\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.75,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.59,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.73,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.73,\n      \"feedback\": \"Both equations conserve atoms, and Pulletikurti et al. (2022) supports cyanide-assisted ketoacid chemistry involving hydantoin-family and N-carbamoylglycine products. However, rxn_014 is a lumped transformation whose particular mechanism and reagent requirements are not documented in the reaction entry. The supplied literature supports heated processing, not unrestricted surface conversion and decarbamoylation. Missing explicit hydantoin nodes are not inherently invalid, but the lumped representation needs experimental justification and an established route to free glycine.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Link rxn_014 to the specific experimental formulation and explain the hydantoin/carbamoyl intermediates, either explicitly or through a validated lumped mechanism.\",\n        \"Specify ammonia source, cyanide concentration, any additional required reagents, pH and heating conditions.\",\n        \"Demonstrate N-carbamoylglycine release to free glycine under the proposed conditions, separately from analytical work-up.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.66,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.57\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.64,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.76,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.55,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.69,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.66,\n      \"feedback\": \"Zhang et al. (2017) supports metal-assisted hydrothermal ethanolamine-to-glycine conversion, including activity with iron, but not definitive validation of this exact two-step mechanism. With rxn_016 used twice and the inferred rxn_017 coefficients, the net conversion is ethanolamine + water -> glycine + 2 H2; no external oxidant is required by conservation. Nevertheless, hydrogen evolution must be established, and aminoacetaldehyde disproportionation competes with condensation chemistry because this intermediate is unstable and enolizable. The speculative labeling is appropriate.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify metal state, alkaline pH, temperature, pressure and ethanolamine concentration against the reported approximately 0.1 M, 160\u00b0C experiments.\",\n        \"Measure aminoacetaldehyde, hydrogen and ethanolamine recycling to distinguish the proposed mechanism from alternative metal-mediated oxidation pathways.\",\n        \"Record the two-aminoacetaldehyde coefficient and evaluate hydrogen back-pressure, metal corrosion or passivation, and glycine survival.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.74\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.74,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.55,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.75,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.71,\n      \"feedback\": \"The balanced net equation permits internal redox conversion without an external oxidant. Weber (1998) supports low-yield glycine formation from glyoxal in thiol-containing mixtures, so this branch has experimental precedent. However, the thiol identity, loading, availability and fate are absent from the submitted environment description. The proposed thioester chemistry also makes it important to distinguish free glycine from a bound precursor pool. The reported approximately 0.41% yield demonstrates formation but does not establish substantial network flux.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the thiol as a reaction agent, including concentration, pH, temperature and environmental availability.\",\n        \"Establish whether the thiol is regenerated; if consumed, expand the reaction representation to include its actual products.\",\n        \"Resolve thioester intermediates and any required hydrolysis, and quantify free-glycine production against competing carbonyl reactions.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"Network-level a