l constraint, although the actual concentration, buffer identity, and operating conditions are not included in the reaction entry. Supplied glyoxylate is accepted as a declared boundary resource.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_011"], "suggestions": ["Attach an explicit recipe and citation, such as the reported approximately 0.5 M glyoxylate/ammonium-buffer system at 50 degrees C for 48 hours.", "Specify buffer identity, pH, and maintenance of concentration; do not extrapolate the reported conversion to dilute or unbuffered solutions."]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_012": 0.92}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.92, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.75, "target_achievement": 0.95, "overall_confidence": 0.88, "feedback": "Kitadai et al. (2019) directly support glyoxylate reductive amination by an electrochemically pre-reduced FeS-Fe0 assemblage. The network correctly treats low-valence iron as a consumed reductant rather than a perpetually active catalyst. Fe(OH)2 is acceptable as the declared accounting equivalent, not an experimentally identified corrosion phase. The remaining uncertainty concerns the active material's preparation, loading, preservation, and aqueous operating conditions; the label 'Biochemical' does not establish those conditions.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_012"], "suggestions": ["Document the active assemblage preparation, including the sufficiently reducing pretreatment and accessible Fe0 inventory.", "Specify anoxic handling and a literature-matched operating window, approximately pH 9.6 and 23-25 degrees C for 48 hours in the cited experiment."]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_013": 0.92}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.92, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.74, "target_achievement": 0.95, "overall_confidence": 0.87, "feedback": "Hydrogen-driven reductive amination over active nickel-bearing metal is supported by Kaur et al. (2024). The net equation correctly uses H2 as the reductant and does not require nickel consumption. However, generic nickel content does not establish catalytic activity, and the submission does not provide hydrogen pressure, catalyst preparation, or solution composition. Competition with hydroxy-acid formation affects selectivity but does not invalidate experimentally observed glycine production.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_013"], "suggestions": ["Identify the actual active nickel-bearing material, its loading and surface preparation, and controls demonstrating catalytic activity.", "Specify pressure, pH, ammonium source, and residence time against the reported approximately 5 bar H2, pH 8, 22 degrees C, 72-hour experimental anchor."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_014": 0.93}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.93, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.76, "target_achievement": 0.95, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "Chimiak et al. (2024) provide direct experimental support for ammonia-fed ferroan-brucite glycine synthesis. Two Fe(II) equivalents per reductive amination correctly supply the required electrons. The component-equivalent treatment is acceptable and does not imply that rxn_012 corrosion products are active brucite. The main gaps are mineral composition/loading, reactant concentrations, reaction duration, and product partitioning. Mineral-associated glycine still satisfies the molecular target, but should be distinguished from dissolved product.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_014"], "suggestions": ["Specify ferroan-brucite composition, accessible Fe(II) loading, pH, temperature, and incubation time.", "Maintain an environment-specific fresh-brucite inventory and report dissolved versus mineral-associated glycine separately."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_015": 0.89, "rxn_014": 0.93}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.9, "pathway_coherence": 0.94, "environmental_progression": 0.71, "target_achievement": 0.93, "overall_confidence": 0.85, "feedback": "The integrated nitrate-to-glycine system is directly supported by Chimiak et al. (2024), rather than merely inferred by joining unrelated experiments. The eight-Fe(II) nitrate-reduction coefficient is chemically appropriate; combining both reactions requires ten Fe(II) equivalents per glycine at ideal stoichiometry. Important implementation constraints are the approximately 100 mM reactants, six-week incubation, modest conversion, and substantial mineral association. These observations establish glycine formation but not a large freely dissolved glycine inventory.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_015", "rxn_014"], "suggestions": ["Provide a coupled nitrogen and electron budget, including accessible mineral Fe(II), nitrate conversion, ammonia availability, and competing reduction products.", "Specify concentration maintenance and six-week mineral/reactant retention under the intended aqueous conditions.", "Distinguish directly dissolved glycine from mineral-associated glycine recovered during work-up; do not count the latter automatically as freely available product."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P6", "overall_confidence": 0.88, "network_feedback": "The network has sufficient nodes, consistent molecular identities, and a connected reaction graph. The deterministic conservation check is accepted: eight reactions are balanced as listed and seven are balanceable with reasonable coefficients; none violates atom or charge conservation. Atmospheric reactions are acceptable lumped precursor-supply equations, not elementary mechanisms or quantitative yield predictions. Literature alignment is strong for the Strecker, aza-Cannizzaro, FeS-Fe0, H2/Ni, and ferroan-brucite systems. Several supported branches genuinely produce glycine, and the explicitly conditional hydroxylamine branch does not negate those routes. Declared feedstock and mineral inventories are accepted as boundary resources, without assuming upstream closure or mineral regeneration. Nevertheless, the supplied reaction entries lack the numerical operating conditions, reaction-level citations, and physical transfer specifications needed to verify the complete implementation. Hydrolysis kinetics and preservation, active mineral preparation, and dissolved-versus-mineral-associated product accounting remain important gaps. P3 and P4 were evaluated through the imine-first branch because their reaction sequences were not explicitly enumerated. Flagged reactions require additional substantiation or condition specification; none is flagged for conservation failure. Route count, shared hubs, and environmental ordering were not scoring advantages or disadvantages. The network is credible but does not meet the 0.90 network-approval threshold as submitted."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 214645.37382125854, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:06:21.670162", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glycine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Glycine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Glycine: Routes, Intermediates, Catalysts, Environments, and Unresolved Problems\n\n## Scope and principal conclusions\n\nGlycine is unusually important for origin-of-life chemistry because it is both the simplest proteinogenic \u03b1-amino acid and one of the most reproducibly generated amino acids in abiotic experiments. The literature does **not** point to a single uniquely compelling prebiotic route. Instead, glycine sits at the intersection of several chemically distinct networks: aldehyde\u2013cyanide chemistry, HCN polymer chemistry, glyoxylate reduction and disproportionation, high-energy atmospheric chemistry, impact chemistry, mineral-mediated hydrothermal chemistry, photocatalysis, amino-alcohol oxidation, and extraterrestrial ice chemistry. Many experiments that are colloquially described as \u201cglycine synthesis\u201d actually produce **acid-hydrolyzable glycine precursors** rather than free glycine in the primary reaction mixture; this distinction is particularly important for high-energy particle, hydrothermal, HCN-polymer, and some early atmospheric experiments.\n\nThe most experimentally mature route at the level of elementary organic chemistry is the **Strecker network**:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2CO + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons CH_2{=}NH + H_2O}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_2{=}NH + HCN/CN^- \\rightarrow NH_2CH_2CN}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH_2CN \\xrightarrow{H_2O} NH_2CH_2CONH_2 \\rightarrow NH_2CH_2COOH}\n\\]\n\nwith **formaldehyde**, **HCN/cyanide**, **ammonia**, **aminoacetonitrile** and **glycinamide** as the central feedstocks/intermediates. An equivalent branch begins with HCN addition to formaldehyde to form **glycolonitrile, HOCH\u2082CN**, followed by ammoniation to aminoacetonitrile. This chemistry requires no mineral catalyst in principle. Miller's discharge experiments supplied the carbonyl, cyanide and ammonia chemistry upstream; hydrothermal experiments have instead added those compounds directly.\n\nNo environmental setting, however, simultaneously solves all of the supply, concentration, kinetics, and compatibility problems. Classical CH\u2084-rich atmospheric synthesis has high laboratory productivity but is sensitive to atmospheric redox state; aqueous Strecker synthesis needs concentrated HCN and carbonyl compounds; high-temperature hydrothermal synthesis competes with amino-acid degradation; recent glyoxylate/mineral systems use more metabolism-like carbon skeletons but require a credible source and concentration mechanism for glyoxylate and fixed nitrogen; surface photochemistry requires appropriate UV, solutes, and photocatalysts; impact and extraterrestrial routes are demonstrably productive but episodic or external. Geological constraints on the atmosphere remain debated: Hadean zircons have been interpreted as indicating mantle oxygen fugacities broadly comparable to later Earth, while impact models permit transient strongly reducing atmospheres and a recent 2026 study has argued for a much more reduced late-Hadean atmosphere. Those are competing models rather than a settled environmental boundary condition.\n\n### Comparative route map\n\n| Route family | Immediate C/N precursors to glycine | Principal energy or reductant | Representative glycine result | Main prebiotic concern |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Atmospheric electric discharge | H\u2082CO + HCN + NH\u2083 \u2192 aminoacetonitrile | Lightning/spark | Miller-type CH\u2084 experiments give percent-level total amino-acid carbon yields; Miller's archived apparatus gave glycine yields reported around 0.4\u20132.1% of methane carbon depending on apparatus. | Strongly reducing bulk atmosphere is contested; neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmospheres give far lower yields. |\n| Volcanic/H\u2082S discharge | Same Strecker feedstocks plus sulfur radical chemistry | Lightning, reduced volcanic gases | Glycine and a much broader amino-acid inventory recovered from Miller's H\u2082S experiments. | Requires localized coexistence of sufficiently reduced gases and intense electrical discharge. |\n| Proton/cosmic-ray atmospheric chemistry | CO or CH\u2084 + N\u2082 + H\u2082O \u2192 complex precursors | MeV protons | CO/N\u2082/H\u2082O gave glycine-equivalent yield \u22482 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 mol J\u207b\u00b9 after hydrolysis. | Much glycine is liberated only by strong acid hydrolysis; natural integrated radiation flux is much smaller than accelerator flux. |\n| Classical aqueous Strecker | H\u2082CO, HCN, NH\u2083 | Chemical free energy; heat for hydrolysis | Robust in solution and under hydrothermal treatment. | HCN/H\u2082CO concentrations and aminonitrile hydrolysis are environmental bottlenecks. |\n| HCN condensation/freezing | HCN/NH\u2084CN \u2192 nitrile/polymer network | Heat or long-duration freezing | Or\u00f3\u2013Kamat HCN chemistry and frozen NH\u2084CN systems generate glycine or glycine precursors. | Efficient HCN polymerization generally needs substantial concentration; dilute oceanic HCN hydrolyzes instead. |\n| Cyanosulfidic photochemistry | HCN-derived glycolonitrile/aldehydes + reduced N | UV + H\u2082S, often Cu photoredox | Produces \u03b1-aminonitriles that are Strecker precursors of glycine and other amino acids. | Co-location of cyanide, sulfide, soluble Cu chemistry and suitable UV remains debated. |\n| Formaldehyde + hydroxylamine | H\u2082CO + NH\u2082OH \u2192 oxime \u2192 HCN/NH\u2083/nitriles | Heat; metals may alter selectivity | Glycine is a major product; cyanide, aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide appear before amino acids. | Prebiotic abundance of hydroxylamine is poorly constrained. |\n| Formaldehyde + ammonia | H\u2082CO + NH\u2083 \u2192 complex N-organic polymer | Heat | Glycine is predominant after hydrolysis among seven amino acids. | Product is initially bound material; mechanism and environmental concentration are uncertain. |\n| Glyoxylate\u2013NH\u2083 N-acyl route | Glyoxylate + NH\u2083 \u2192 N-oxalylglycine | Acidic water; subsequent hydrolysis | 5\u201339% glycine after 6 M HCl hydrolysis. | Product liberation required laboratory-strength acid; pH optimum 3\u20134. |\n| Glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro | Glyoxylate + NH\u2084\u207a \u2192 imine/hemiaminal pair | Internal hydride transfer | ~11\u201314% glycine at 0.5 M glyoxylate, 50\u00b0C, pH 7\u201310. | Strong concentration dependence; little reaction below ~0.25 M glyoxylate. |\n| Glyoxylate reductive amination | Glyoxylate + NH\u2083 \u2192 imine \u2192 glycine | Fe(II)/Fe\u2070 or H\u2082/Ni | Electroreduced FeS assemblage: 59%; H\u2082/Ni systems also reduce glyoxylate under mild conditions. | Requires specific reducing mineral/metal states and a glyoxylate supply. |\n| Nitrate + glyoxylate + ferroan brucite | NO\u2083\u207b \u2192 NH\u2083; glyoxylate \u2192 imine \u2192 glycine | Fe(II) in Mg\u2013Fe hydroxide | Nitrate system ~0.1\u20133.1% glycine after 6 weeks; corresponding NH\u2083 systems 52\u201369%. | 100 mM-scale reactants and long times; most nitrate-route glycine can remain mineral-bound. |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs/ketoacid chemistry | Glyoxylate + CN\u207b + NH\u2083 source \u2192 hydantoin \u2192 glycine | Heat | Glyoxylate-derived hydantoins gave glycine at tens-of-percent levels under heated hydrolysis conditions. | Still cyanide-dependent; some implementations use diamidophosphate; heating required. |\n| TiO\u2082 nitrate/methanol photocatalysis | CH\u2083OH \u2192 ethylene glycol \u2192 glyoxylate; NO\u2083\u207b \u2192 NH\u2083 | 365-nm light, anatase TiO\u2082/Ba\u00b2\u207a | Up to 870 \u03bcmol glycine gcat\u207b\u00b9 h\u207b\u00b9. | Demonstrated with engineered nanocatalyst and concentrated methanol/nitrate; direct Hadean analog remains speculative. |\n| Ethanolamine oxidation | HOCH\u2082CH\u2082NH\u2082 \u2192 aminoacetaldehyde \u2192 glycine | Metal + alkaline hydrothermal conditions | Up to 23.5% with Co at pH 12, 160\u00b0C; far less at dilute ethanolamine. | Origin and 0.1 M accumulation of ethanolamine; native metal availability. |\n| Thiol/formose route | Glyoxal/glycolaldehyde/glyoxylate + NH\u2083 + thiol | Carbonyl disproportionation | Glyoxal gave ~0.41% glycine; glycolaldehyde + glyoxylate ~0.18%. | Requires already-formed C\u2082 carbonyls and thiols; yields are low. |\n| Impact/shock chemistry | NH\u2084HCOO or NH\u2084HCO\u2083; cometary CH\u2083OH/CO\u2082/NH\u2083 ice | 10\u201350 GPa shock | Ammonium formate: maximum glycine ~8.5 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074 mol%; comet-ice shocks also form amino acids after hydrolysis. | Very low conversion and simultaneous thermal destruction; laboratory shock histories differ from natural impacts. |\n| Interstellar/cometary ice photochemistry | H\u2082O, CH\u2083OH, NH\u2083, HCN or CH\u2083NH\u2082/CO\u2082 | UV/cosmic radiation at ~10\u201315 K | Glycine repeatedly detected after irradiation and warm-up. | Exogenous rather than terrestrial synthesis; delivered fraction and survival are separate questions. |\n| Formamide + meteorite/protons | HCONH\u2082 | High-energy protons + meteoritic minerals | Chondrite experiments averaged ~1.12 \u03bcg mL\u207b\u00b9 glycine plus N-formylglycine. | Accumulation of bulk liquid formamide on early Earth is unresolved. |\n| Gas-phase/network-theory CO route | CO + H\u2082O + NH\u2083, optionally H\u2082 | Thermal chemistry | Computationally identified routes to formaldimine \u2192 glycine, but barriers ~50\u201360+ kcal mol\u207b\u00b9. | No equivalent experimental demonstration; calculated barriers are extremely high at ordinary prebiotic temperatures. |\n\n## Cyanide, atmospheric, photochemical, and high-energy routes\n\n### Electric-discharge production and the Strecker network\n\nStanley L. Miller's 1953 *Science* experiment established the foundational result: electrical discharge through an aqueous/gas system containing CH\u2084, NH\u2083, H\u2082 and water vapor generated glycine and other amino acids. Miller's later mechanistic work showed why the experiment works chemically: electrical discharge first generates **HCN and aldehydes**, including formaldehyde, after which amino-acid formation is largely accounted for by Strecker-type chemistry rather than by direct assembly of an amino acid in the gas phase.\n\nThis distinction matters for network design. For glycine, the important elementary inventory is therefore not merely CH\u2084 + NH\u2083 + H\u2082O, but:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_4/N_2/NH_3/H_2O \\xrightarrow{discharge} HCN + H_2CO + reduced\\ N}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2CO + NH_3 + HCN \\rightarrow NH_2CH_2CN}\n\\]\n\nand aqueous hydrolysis to glycine. Formaldehyde can also trap HCN as glycolonitrile, which is then converted to aminoacetonitrile. This route does **not require a mineral catalyst** once formaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia coexist; the energetic burden is predominantly upstream feedstock generation and, downstream, nitrile hydrolysis.\n\nSchlesinger and Miller's 1983 *Journal of Molecular Evolution* systematic atmosphere experiments showed the strong dependence on redox state. CH\u2084-containing mixtures gave total amino-acid carbon yields of roughly 1.2\u20134.7%, whereas glycine was almost the only amino acid produced efficiently when CO or CO\u2082 replaced methane. Their companion work found HCN and formaldehyde yields as high as approximately 10\u201313% of carbon under sufficiently reducing CH\u2084 or H\u2082-rich CO/CO\u2082 conditions, but synthesis declined sharply when H\u2082/CO or H\u2082/CO\u2082 became too small.\n\nThe archival reanalysis by Adam P. Johnson, H. James Cleaves, Jason P. Dworkin, Daniel P. Glavin, Antonio Lazcano and Jeffrey L. Bada (2008, *Science*) is quantitatively useful. Miller's \u201cvolcanic\u201d apparatus contained 200 torr CH\u2084, 200 torr NH\u2083 and 100 torr H\u2082 over 500 mL water and was sparked for about a week. Reanalysis found 22 amino acids and five amines in the volcanic experiment. Miller's original quantitative records corresponded to glycine yields of approximately **2.1%, 1.8% and 0.4% of carbon added as methane** in the three apparatus configurations examined. The water-mist volcanic apparatus was chemically richer than the iconic textbook configuration.\n\nEric T. Parker and colleagues (2011, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*) subsequently analyzed Miller's 1958 H\u2082S-containing discharge samples. Glycine again occurred along with numerous other amino and sulfur-containing acids, supporting the narrower proposition that **localized H\u2082S-rich volcanic plumes plus lightning** could supply amino-acid chemistry even if the planet-wide atmosphere were less reducing.\n\nThe geological critique is substantial. Hadean zircon-based redox work by Trail and co-workers has been interpreted to indicate that mantle-derived Hadean melts had oxygen fugacities not radically different from later terrestrial mantle, disfavoring the assumption of a persistent global CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere. Neutral N\u2082\u2013CO\u2082 atmospheres are much less productive in spark chemistry. However, the critique no longer implies that reduced atmospheric chemistry was impossible: impact-generated H\u2082/CH\u2084 atmospheres, reduced volcanic microenvironments, and transient post-impact atmospheres provide mechanisms for episodic Miller-like chemistry. Thus the contested point is **scale and persistence**, not whether the reactions themselves work.\n\n### CO\u2013N\u2082 atmospheres and energetic particles\n\nA distinct atmospheric framework avoids the need for abundant methane. Kensei Kobayashi and colleagues irradiated **350 torr CO + 350 torr \u00b9\u2075N\u2082 over 5 mL water** with 2.5\u20133.0 MeV protons at 297 K for 3 h. After 6 M HCl hydrolysis at 100\u00b0C for 24 h, glycine and other organics were recovered. The glycine energy yield was approximately **2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 mol J\u207b\u00b9**, comparable to proton irradiation of CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O and about twice the cited energy-normalized glycine output of a CH\u2084/N\u2082/NH\u2083/H\u2082O spark experiment.\n\nEarlier Kensei Kobayashi, Takeo Kaneko, Takeshi Saito and Tairo Oshima experiments likewise found glycine yields proportional to energy deposition, with a **G-value of about 0.02 molecules per 100 eV** near a CO:N\u2082 ratio of 1.\n\nThis pathway is mechanistically less resolved than Strecker synthesis. High-energy ionization dissociates the very strong CO and N\u2082 bonds, producing radical/ionic C\u2013N\u2013O networks and HCN among the products. Crucially, much of the analytical \u201camino acid yield\u201d appears only **after acid hydrolysis**, so the primary products are likely macromolecular or nitrile/amide precursors rather than free glycine. That makes this route interesting for feedstock generation but prevents a simple one-step mapping \u201cCO + N\u2082 \u2192 free glycine.\u201d\n\nIts environmental advantage is use of N\u2082 rather than pre-existing NH\u2083. Its weakness is energetic scaling: accelerator experiments use very high instantaneous proton fluxes, and estimates of globally integrated early-solar/cosmic-particle production depend strongly on young-Sun particle spectra, atmospheric shielding and atmospheric composition. Recent proton-irradiation experiments continue to find amino-acid precursors in CO\u2082/CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O atmospheres with only small amounts of methane, reinforcing the chemistry while leaving planetary production rates model-dependent.\n\n### HCN concentration, polymerization and freezing\n\nJuan Or\u00f3 and S. S. Kamat's 1961 *Nature* experiments demonstrated that concentrated ammoniacal HCN can generate glycine, alanine and other amino-acid products. Related Or\u00f3\u2013Kimball HCN-condensation work recovered glycinamide and glycine from the aqueous products of cyanide polymerization. This is not simply classical Strecker chemistry because polymerized HCN itself creates a network of C\u2013N intermediates that hydrolyze into amino acids and other products.\n\nThe central geochemical problem is concentration. In kinetic treatments cited by experimental workers, HCN polymerization becomes competitive at roughly **\u22650.01 M**, whereas at much lower concentration hydrolysis toward formamide/formate and ammonia dominates. One atmospheric-ocean calculation for a weakly reducing atmosphere estimated a steady-state oceanic HCN concentration of only about \\(8\\times10^{-10}\\) M under its assumptions. Such global-ocean estimates do not apply directly to evaporating ponds, aerosols, ice eutectics or local impact deposits, but they demonstrate why essentially every HCN-based origin model requires a **concentration mechanism**.\n\nLevy, Miller, Brinton and Bada (2000, *Icarus*) supplied one such mechanism experimentally: dilute NH\u2084CN frozen for years at \u221220 to \u221278\u00b0C continued reacting in the eutectic liquid between ice crystals, and simple amino acids dominated by glycine accumulated. Freezing therefore solves a real kinetic/concentration problem without evaporation, but it shifts the environmental requirement toward persistent cold traps and long reaction times.\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry\n\nBhavesh H. Patel, Claudia Percivalle, Dougal J. Ritson, Colm D. Duffy and John D. Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*) developed a broader **cyanosulfidic protometabolism** in which HCN supplies carbon and nitrogen, H\u2082S supplies reducing equivalents, UV light supplies energy, and copper cyanide photochemistry can accelerate electron transfer. The network gives glycolaldehyde/glyceraldehyde chemistry and **\u03b1-aminonitriles**, including the immediate Strecker-class precursors from which glycine and other amino acids arise by hydrolysis.\n\nFor glycine network design, this pathway is best regarded as an **upstream synthesis of glycolonitrile/aminoacetonitrile-class material**, rather than evidence that a pond irradiated with UV simply accumulates large amounts of isolated glycine. Its strength is systems compatibility: the same photochemical network can generate precursors associated with amino acids, nucleotides and lipids. Its contested aspect is geochemical co-location. Significant cyanide, reduced sulfur, appropriate copper speciation and UV transmission must overlap spatially and temporally; high sulfide can also strongly alter metal speciation. The laboratory's narrowband lamps are experimental proxies rather than exact reproductions of the Hadean solar spectrum, although follow-up photochemistry has shown that relevant cyanocuprate electron photodetachment overlaps wavelengths expected to have reached an anoxic early-Earth surface.\n\n## Carbonyl, glyoxylate, nitrogen-oxide, and mineral routes\n\n### Formaldehyde plus hydroxylamine\n\nJuan Or\u00f3, A. P. Kimball, Stanley Fritz and Francis Master (1959, *Archives of Biochemistry and Biophysics*) showed that heating formaldehyde with hydroxylamine gives a suite of amino and hydroxy acids, with glycine prominent. Mechanistically this is especially interesting because externally added HCN is unnecessary: **formaldoxime** and related N\u2013O intermediates generate HCN and NH\u2083 internally, after which Strecker/cyanohydrin chemistry can take over.\n\nKamaluddin, Hiroshi Yanagawa and F. Egami (1979, *Journal of Biochemistry*) monitored this chemistry in a \u201cmodified sea medium.\u201d They found cyanide, **glycinenitrile/aminoacetonitrile** and **glycinamide** before amino acids accumulated, giving experimental support to the sequence:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2CO + NH_2OH \\rightarrow CH_2{=}NOH}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{oxime\\ chemistry \\rightarrow HCN + NH_3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCN + H_2CO + NH_3 \\rightarrow NH_2CH_2CN\n\\rightarrow glycinamide \\rightarrow glycine}.\n\\]\n\nHiroshi Hatanaka and Egami's 1977 experiments heated formaldehyde/hydroxylamine media near **pH 5.5 and 105\u00b0C** with mixtures of Zn\u00b2\u207a, Fe\u00b3\u207a, Cu\u00b2\u207a, Co\u00b2\u207a, Mn\u00b2\u207a, molybdate and clay minerals. Glycine, alanine, serine and \u03b2-alanine were among the confirmed products. Molybdate changed amino-acid selectivity rather than simply increasing everything: increasing molybdate favored several other amino acids while **decreasing glycine and serine**. Thus this is an experimentally demonstrated example in which a mineral/metal component controls network branching rather than serving as a generic \u201ccatalyst.\u201d\n\nThe major unresolved issue is upstream **hydroxylamine supply**. N\u2013O compounds are chemically attractive fixed-nitrogen intermediates, but no consensus inventory demonstrates that hydroxylamine would have reached the concentrations used in these experiments in a particular Hadean environment. The metal-rich \u201cmodified seawater\u201d recipes also should not automatically be identified with real Hadean seawater.\n\n### Formaldehyde plus ammonia without externally supplied cyanide\n\nSidney W. Fox and C. R. Windsor (1970, *Science*) showed that heating formaldehyde with ammonia generates an organic product which, after hydrolysis, yields seven amino acids; **glycine was predominant**. This establishes an experimentally independent H\u2082CO/NH\u2083 route in which no cyanide is added.\n\nIts interpretation must be conservative. The immediate reaction product is complex bound organic matter, and glycine appears after hydrolysis, so it is better represented as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2CO + NH_3 \\xrightarrow{heat} N\\!-\\!organic\\ polymer/precursors\n\\xrightarrow{hydrolysis} glycine}\n\\]\n\nrather than as a selective one-step glycine synthesis. The route demonstrates that formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry has more than one path to a glycine skeleton, but its detailed reaction network and environmental concentration requirements are much less constrained than classical Strecker chemistry.\n\n### The older glyoxylate\u2013ammonia N-acyl route\n\nHiroshi Yanagawa, Y. Makino, K. Sato, M. Nishizawa and F. Egami (1984, *Origins of Life*) discovered that glyoxylic acid and ammonia form **N-oxalylglycine** in aqueous solution, with an optimum near **pH 3\u20134**. Subsequent hydrolysis with 6 N HCl released glycine in **5\u201339% yield**.\n\nThat route is mechanistically and prebiotically distinct from reductive amination. It establishes a C\u2082 glyoxylate/ammonia pathway but does **not** show facile accumulation of free glycine under the reaction conditions because strong-acid hydrolysis was used to liberate the amino acid. For a synthesis-network model, N-oxalylglycine should therefore be represented as a potentially stable reservoir/intermediate, with the hydrolysis step treated as a separate environmental problem.\n\n### Catalyst-free aza-Cannizzaro glycine formation\n\nDean R. Jarois, Lars E. Schimmelpfennig and Samuel H. Gellman (2024, *Chemistry\u2014A European Journal*) uncovered a fundamentally different glyoxylate mechanism. In concentrated ammonium solutions, glyoxylate forms imine/hemiaminal species that undergo intermolecular **hydride transfer** analogous to a Cannizzaro disproportionation. Glycine and oxamic acid are produced without an external reductant or metal catalyst.\n\nAt **0.5 M glyoxylic acid + 0.5 M ammonium phosphate, 50\u00b0C, 48 h**, glycine yield was about 11%. Reported yields were roughly 2.0% at pH 5, 2.4% at pH 6, 10.7% at pH 7, 12.7% at pH 8, 12.1% at pH 9 and 13.8% at pH 10; ammonium acetate and sulfate also supported the reaction. Isotopic experiments support transfer of glyoxylate-derived hydrogen to the glycine product.\n\nThe key negative result is equally important: little reaction occurred at glyoxylate concentrations below roughly **0.25 M** on the experimental timescale, and glyoxylic acid plus a stoichiometric equivalent of NH\u2084OH in unbuffered water did not give the same glycine production. Buffer anions appear to assist proton-transfer chemistry. This is therefore among the cleanest catalyst-free mechanisms known, but also among the routes most clearly dependent on **strong geochemical concentration**, suggesting evaporitic, freezing, aerosol, or pore-space settings rather than a dilute global ocean.\n\n### FeS/Fe\u2070 and alkaline-hydrothermal reductive amination\n\nNorio Kitadai, Ryuhei Nakamura, Masahiro Yamamoto, Ken Takai, Naohiro Yoshida and Yoshi Oono (2019, *Science Advances*) showed that partially electroreduced FeS can be converted into an **FeS\u2013Fe\u2070 assemblage** at potentials \u2264 approximately \u22120.7 V versus SHE. That material drove several protometabolic reductions. In glyoxylate experiments near **pH 9.6, 23\u201325\u00b0C for 48 h**, glycine yield reached **59%**; oxaloacetate gave aspartate at 64%.\n\nThe probable glycine mechanism is standard reductive amination:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CHOCOO^- + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons HN{=}CHCOO^- + H_2O}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by reduction of the imine:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HN{=}CHCOO^- + 2H^+ + 2e^- \\rightarrow NH_2CH_2COO^-}.\n\\]\n\nHere the Fe-bearing phase is not merely a passive adsorption surface; its low-valence iron is the electron source. The geological argument is that H\u2082-rich alkaline hydrothermal systems can establish sufficiently reducing electrochemical potentials across conductive/mineral precipitates.\n\nThe main critique is experimental realization. The particularly active material was **electrochemically pre-reduced in the laboratory**; demonstrating that comparable FeS/Fe\u2070 assemblages form and persist with adequate reactive area in a natural Hadean vent is a separate geochemical problem. The experiment also begins with millimolar glyoxylate and high ammonium, meaning the origin and concentration of those soluble substrates are not solved by the catalyst demonstration itself.\n\nEarlier thermodynamic analysis of the FeS\u2013H\u2082S/FeS\u2082 \u201ciron\u2013sulfur world\u201d by Schoonen, Xu and Bebie (1999) found another important constraint: the reducing power available from the FeS/H\u2082S\u2013pyrite couple declines as temperature rises, while the reducing demand of CO\u2082 fixation increases; direct electron transfer from pyrrhotite to CO\u2082 also has a substantial kinetic barrier. CO, CS\u2082 and formate are more kinetically accessible carbon substrates. This is a published reason not to equate \u201cthermodynamically possible\u201d with \u201ckinetically plausible,\u201d particularly for direct CO\u2082-to-amino-acid proposals.\n\n### H\u2082/Ni reductive amination and protometabolic coupling\n\nHarpreet Kaur, Sophia A. Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William F. Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz and Joseph Moran (2024, *Chem*) demonstrated a complementary system in which **H\u2082 is the reductant and Ni is the catalyst**. A representative condition was about **30 mM ketoacid, pH 8 water, 5 bar H\u2082, ammonium bicarbonate, 22\u00b0C for 72 h**. Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, and Ni-bearing Aletai meteorite material all promoted reduction; controls without metal catalyst produced no reduction products.\n\nAcross the ketoacid set, Ni and meteorite catalysts gave combined amino-acid + hydroxy-acid yields of approximately **6\u201384%**, while Pd gave roughly 52\u201388%; Pd generally favored amino acid over hydroxy acid more strongly than Ni. Ammonium concentrations down into the millimolar range could still support chemistry. In coupled one-pot reaction networks containing glyoxylate and other ketoacids, glycine appeared together with aspartate and alanine.\n\nThe conceptual advantage is biochemical congruence: extant biosynthesis makes amino acids largely by reductive amination/transamination of ketoacid skeletons, not by Strecker addition of cyanide to aldehydes. The limitation is geochemical catalyst realism. Serpentinization readily supplies H\u2082, but the quantity, surface area, poisoning behavior and persistence of **native Ni/Ni-rich metal** accessible to aqueous organics are much less certain than the availability of silicates, sulfides or hydroxides. Pd/C is useful mechanistically but cannot reasonably be treated as an abundant primitive-Earth catalyst.\n\n### Nitrate reduction and ferroan brucite\n\nLucy Chimiak, Emiko Hara, Alex L. Sessions and Alexis S. Templeton (2024, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*) integrated fixed-nitrogen generation and glycine synthesis in a single mineral system. Synthetic **ferroan brucite, Fe\u2080.\u2083\u2083Mg\u2080.\u2086\u2087(OH)\u2082**, was incubated with glyoxylate and nitrate under alkaline, approximately room-temperature conditions. Fe(II) reduces nitrate toward ammonia; ammonia condenses with glyoxylate, and additional Fe(II) reducing equivalents convert the imine to glycine.\n\nWith initial concentrations of approximately **100 mM nitrate and 100 mM glyoxylate**, six-week nitrate experiments produced about **0.1\u20133.1% glycine** relative to initial organic substrate, whereas experiments supplied directly with ammonia yielded roughly **52\u201369%**. A striking 71\u201397% of the glycine generated in nitrate systems could be recovered only after digestion of the mineral fraction, showing strong adsorption or incorporation; corresponding mineral-bound fractions in NH\u2083 experiments were lower, about 17\u201327%.\n\nThis system addresses one objection to earlier reductive-amination routes\u2014namely the assumption that ammonia was already available\u2014but does so at the cost of introducing nitrate. Whether Hadean nitrate production and accumulation could sustain ~0.1 M local concentrations is therefore the key environmental question. The modest nitrate-to-glycine yield and six-week timescale also matter for network flux calculations, while mineral sequestration could be either a liability or a useful concentration/protection mechanism.\n\n### Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion of glyoxylate and related ketoacids\n\nSunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen and Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*) showed that \u03b1-ketoacid skeletons can be converted to amino acids through **Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry** using cyanide and ammonia sources. For glyoxylate, hydantoin-family intermediates are generated and then hydrolyzed to glycine; heated reactions around **80\u00b0C** produced glycine at reported tens-of-percent levels, alongside N-carbamoylglycine.\n\nThis route occupies an intermediate conceptual position between Strecker chemistry and metabolism-first chemistry. The carbon skeleton is a biologically recognizable ketoacid rather than an aldehyde, but cyanide is still a key reagent. The study was explicitly motivated by the discontinuity between classical prebiotic Strecker chemistry and extant transamination chemistry and demonstrated that cyanide chemistry can act directly on metabolic-type ketoacids.\n\nIts contested features are therefore different from those of Ni/H\u2082 reductive amination: it avoids specialized metallic catalysts but retains the cyanide-concentration problem, and some implementations employ **diamidophosphate** as an ammonia/phosphoryl source whose prebiotic inventory is itself debated. Hydantoin hydrolysis also requires thermal processing.\n\n### TiO\u2082 photocatalysis from methanol and nitrate\n\nPeifeng Li, Wanghui Zhao, Kaixuan Wang, Tao Wang and Biaobiao Zhang (2024, *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*) reported a striking modern demonstration of a fully integrated carbon/nitrogen photochemical route:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3OH \\rightarrow HOCH_2CH_2OH \\rightarrow OHC{-}COOH}\n\\]\n\nwhile simultaneously\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NO_3^- \\rightarrow NH_3}\n\\]\n\nand then\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{glyoxylate + NH_3 \\rightarrow imine \\rightarrow glycine}.\n\\]\n\nWith anatase TiO\u2082 modified by Ba\u00b2\u207a, the optimized glycine formation rate reached **870 \u03bcmol gcat\u207b\u00b9 h\u207b\u00b9**. Representative experiments used 5 mg photocatalyst, 4 mmol methanol and 1 mmol nitrate in 10 mL water at about **50\u00b0C, 1 bar Ar, 365-nm illumination**. Glycol/ethylene glycol, glyoxylate and ammonia were experimentally detected as intermediates.\n\nChemically, this is significant because it joins two difficult tasks\u2014C\u2013C formation from a C\u2081 alcohol and nitrate reduction\u2014before reductive amination. As a **prebiotic** route, however, it should presently be classed as proposed rather than established. The optimized system employs synthesized anatase nanoparticles and deliberate Ba\u00b2\u207a modification, ~0.4 M methanol and ~0.1 M nitrate, and monochromatic 365-nm irradiation. Natural TiO\u2082 minerals certainly exist, but equivalence between a laboratory nanocatalyst and a Hadean mineral surface has not yet been demonstrated.\n\n## Hydrothermal, amino-alcohol, formose, and impact chemistry\n\n### Hydrothermal Strecker synthesis\n\nRemy J.-C. Hennet, Nils G. Holm and Michael H. Engel (1992, *Naturwissenschaften*) tested amino-acid formation under hydrothermal conditions. A representative experiment heated an acidified aqueous solution containing roughly **0.19 M formaldehyde, 0.19 M KCN and 0.23 M NH\u2084Cl at 150\u00b0C and about 10 bar**, with Fe sulfide/oxide mineral assemblages and a CO\u2082/H\u2082 gas phase. Glycine was the dominant amino-acid product and occurred at millimolar scale.\n\nThis is good evidence that Strecker chemistry **survives hydrothermal pressure and temperature**; it is not evidence that hydrothermal vents themselves spontaneously produce 0.2 M formaldehyde and cyanide. That distinction is the principal published criticism of many early vent simulations: the organic feedstocks needed by Strecker synthesis were inserted experimentally instead of generated from CO\u2082, N\u2082 and H\u2082 in the same reactor.\n\nHiroshi Yanagawa and Kensei Kobayashi also subjected CH\u2084/N\u2082-bearing \u201cmodified hydrothermal vent medium\u201d to approximately **260\u2013325\u00b0C** and high pressure, recovering glycine, alanine and other amino acids after hydrolysis. Later comparisons concluded that the carbon yields were orders of magnitude below atmospheric-discharge yields. Detection after hydrolysis again indicates that at these temperatures the primary reservoir may be refractory/condensed amino-acid precursors rather than stable dissolved free glycine.\n\nHigh temperature is intrinsically double-edged. Cox and Seward's experimental work on glycine and alanine at roughly 120\u2013165\u00b0C and 20 bar showed dimerization and cyclization competing with persistence of free amino acid, while still hotter sub-/supercritical water promotes both oligomerization and decomposition. Consequently, the most plausible hydrothermal versions of glycine synthesis are generally cooler mixing zones, porous mineral interfaces or circulation systems rather than continuous residence in a 300\u2013400\u00b0C vent fluid.\n\n### Ethanolamine oxidation\n\nXianlong Zhang, Ge Tian, Jing Gao, Mei Han, Rui Su, Yanxiang Wang and Shouhua Feng (2017, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) demonstrated a chemically different hydrothermal route in which **ethanolamine** is oxidized to glycine. Their proposed sequence is metal-assisted dehydrogenation to 2-aminoacetaldehyde followed by alkaline disproportionation/oxidation to glycine.\n\nUsing 0.1 M ethanolamine, pH 9.7 and 160\u00b0C for 6 h, reported glycine yields included approximately **3.1% with Ni, 5.0% with Fe, 5.0% with Cu and 6.8% with Co**; no glycine was detected in the metal-free control. Raising the pH to 12 increased the Co-assisted 160\u00b0C yield to approximately **23.5%**. Pressure increased the yield, and extending reaction time increased conversion.\n\nThe concentration dependence is a serious qualification. At 160\u00b0C under the study's pressure conditions, 0.1 M ethanolamine gave roughly 7% glycine in one condition, whereas 0.01 and 0.005 M gave only traces and 0.001 M gave none. Thus, before this can be treated as a viable origin pathway, an upstream prebiotic route must generate and locally concentrate ethanolamine to tens-of-millimolar or higher levels. The requirement for reactive native Co/Fe/Ni/Cu surfaces under strongly alkaline, hot conditions is another environmental assumption.\n\n### Thiol-assisted formose chemistry\n\nArthur L. Weber (1998, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*) developed a sulfur-containing alternative in which formose-family carbonyls react with ammonia in the presence of thiols. The broader chemistry primarily generated alanine and homoserine, but glycine appeared from particular substrates: **glycolaldehyde + glyoxylate gave about 0.18% glycine**, while **glyoxal gave ~0.41% glycine** under the reported dilute carbonyl/thiol conditions.\n\nThe proposed intermediates are \u03b1-hydroxy/\u03b1-keto aldehydes and **amino-acid thioesters**, making the route conceptually attractive because a product of amino-acid synthesis is already activated toward condensation chemistry. Reactions were conducted under relatively mild aqueous conditions; the related alanine system, for example, operated at 40\u00b0C with 20 mM-scale formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, NH\u2084Cl and a mercaptocarboxylic acid.\n\nFor glycine specifically, however, yields are low and the route does not solve the origin of glyoxal/glyoxylate or thiols. It is best represented as a **secondary network branch** that could transform a pre-existing formose/carbonyl inventory rather than a leading bottom-up route from CO\u2082, CO or CH\u2084.\n\n### Shock chemistry in oceans and impact environments\n\nChizuka Suzuki, Yoshihiro Furukawa, Takamichi Kobayashi, Toshimori Sekine, Hiromoto Nakazawa and Takeshi Kakegawa (2015, *Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems*) shocked aqueous ammonium formate and ammonium bicarbonate at projectile velocities of 0.51\u20130.92 km s\u207b\u00b9, corresponding to approximately **10\u201318 GPa** in the relevant experiments. Glycine, \u03b2-alanine, sarcosine and trace alanine formed from ammonium formate; smaller glycine amounts formed from ammonium bicarbonate.\n\nThe maximum glycine yield from ammonium formate was only about **0.00085 mol% of formate**, and the bicarbonate system was lower still. That low yield is nevertheless mechanistically notable because the feedstocks\u2014formate/bicarbonate and ammonium\u2014are simpler and potentially more geochemically accessible than aminonitriles. No specialized catalyst was intentionally required; mechanical shock supplies the activation.\n\nCometary-ice impact experiments provide a related but distinct scenario. Zita Martins and collaborators showed that hypervelocity shock of mixtures containing H\u2082O ice, CO\u2082, NH\u2083 and CH\u2083OH produces several amino acids after hydrolysis at pressures around tens of GPa. In those experiments methanol and sufficiently high shock pressure were required.\n\nThe difficulty is extrapolation. Natural impacts reach higher temperatures, larger spatial gradients and different cooling histories than millimeter-scale recovery cells. Strong shocks can synthesize and destroy organics simultaneously. Impact chemistry is therefore well supported as an **episodic source**, but present data do not establish that it dominated Earth's integrated glycine budget.\n\n## Extraterrestrial and cosmochemical production\n\n### Ultraviolet irradiation of interstellar ice\n\nMax P. Bernstein, Jason P. Dworkin, Scott A. Sandford, George W. Cooper and Louis J. Allamandola (2002, *Nature*) irradiated interstellar-ice analogues at approximately **15 K**. Ice mixtures were dominated by water with smaller CH\u2083OH, NH\u2083 and HCN fractions; an example composition was approximately H\u2082O:CH\u2083OH:NH\u2083:HCN = 20:2:1:1. After UV irradiation and warm-up, the organic residue contained glycine, alanine and serine.\n\nIn an independent 2002 *Nature* study, Guillermo M. Mu\u00f1oz Caro, Uwe J. Meierhenrich, W. A. Schutte, Barbier, Arcones Segovia, Rosenbauer, Thiemann, Brack and Greenberg irradiated analogous ice at about **12 K** and recovered 16 amino acids after warm-up/work-up, again including glycine. Independent replication across ice compositions is one of the strongest aspects of the extraterrestrial pathway.\n\nChang-Woo Lee, Joon-Ki Kim, Eui-Seong Moon, Y. C. Minh and Heon Kang (2009, *The Astrophysical Journal*) went further by irradiating H\u2082O/CH\u2083NH\u2082/CO\u2082 ice and detecting glycine-related products in situ, supporting radical mechanisms in which photofragmentation of methylamine creates C/N radical partners that combine with CO\u2082-derived functionality.\n\nThis route has a different relevance from an early-Earth pond experiment: it can contribute **preformed extraterrestrial glycine and precursor organics** through dust, comets and carbonaceous asteroids. It does not identify where Earth's first terrestrial glycine had to form. Laboratory ice compositions, radiation doses, warm-up rates and analytical hydrolysis must also be mapped onto actual molecular-cloud, protoplanetary-disk and asteroid histories before production rates can be extrapolated.\n\n### Asteroidal aqueous Strecker chemistry\n\nCarbonaceous meteorites provide independent evidence that Strecker chemistry occurred somewhere in the early Solar System. E. T. Peltzer, Jeffrey L. Bada, G. Schlesinger and Stanley L. Miller (1984, *Advances in Space Research*) found that Murchison's amino/hydroxy-acid distributions were consistent with aqueous parent-body Strecker chemistry and estimated parent-body ammonia and cyanide concentrations in roughly millimolar ranges.\n\nN. R. Lerner, E. Peterson and S. Chang (1993, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) performed isotope-labeled Strecker experiments using deuterated carbonyl compounds and found substantial retention of carbonyl-derived deuterium in amino acids. The experiments support the feasibility of asteroid-parent-body synthesis from inherited interstellar aldehydes/ketones, NH\u2083 and HCN; they also found products attributable to HCN polymer chemistry.\n\nThis is important environmental evidence because it demonstrates that \u201cprebiotic\u201d chemistry need not all occur on Earth. A plausible glycine inventory could have multiple sources: interstellar radical chemistry, aqueous asteroidal Strecker chemistry, impact processing during delivery, and endogenous Earth synthesis.\n\n### Formamide and meteoritic minerals\n\nRaffaele Saladino and co-workers' 2015 *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* study irradiated **liquid formamide with high-energy protons in the presence of powdered meteorites**. A broad suite of organics formed; glycine and N-formylglycine were particularly prominent amino-acid products. For chondrite-catalyzed experiments, glycine plus its formyl derivative averaged about **1.12 \u03bcg mL\u207b\u00b9**.\n\nThe mineral catalyst is the meteorite itself, with different metal/silicate phases enabling radical and redox chemistry under particle irradiation. The route is chemically impressive but its environmental bottleneck is solvent inventory: producing and accumulating large volumes or films of nearly neat formamide on the early Earth is considerably harder to demonstrate than producing trace atmospheric formamide. Irradiation of nitriles near radioactive mineral deposits has been proposed as one source, but estimated production rates are strongly locality-dependent.\n\n## Intermediates, catalysts, and conditions that matter most\n\nAcross otherwise competing frameworks, a surprisingly small set of intermediates recurs. This convergence is useful for synthesis-network design because environmental models can be compared at the level of **how effectively they generate the same nodes**.\n\n| Network node | Role in glycine synthesis | Principal routes feeding it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Formaldehyde, H\u2082CO** | C\u2081 aldehyde for Strecker glycine; precursor to oximes, formose carbonyls and methanol-derived chemistry | Discharge of CH\u2084/CO/CO\u2082 atmospheres; photochemistry; formaldehyde feedstock experiments. |\n| **HCN/CN\u207b** | Nucleophile/carbon donor in Strecker; polymer precursor; cyanosulfidic feedstock | Atmospheric discharge/photochemistry, energetic particles, impact chemistry. |\n| **Glycolonitrile, HOCH\u2082CN** | Stable-ish formaldehyde\u2013cyanide adduct; immediate precursor to aminoacetonitrile chemistry | Classical and cyanosulfidic Strecker networks. |\n| **Aminoacetonitrile, NH\u2082CH\u2082CN** | Canonical nitrile immediately upstream of glycinamide/glycine | Strecker, hydroxylamine/formaldehyde, cyanosulfidic routes. |\n| **Glycinamide** | Hydrolysis intermediate between aminoacetonitrile and glycine | Strecker and hydroxylamine systems. |\n| **Glyoxylate, OHC\u2013COO\u207b** | C\u2082 metabolic-type carbon skeleton; condenses with NH\u2083 to glycine imine | FeS/Fe\u2070, H\u2082/Ni, brucite/nitrate, aza-Cannizzaro, Bucherer\u2013Bergs and TiO\u2082 routes. |\n| **Glyoxylate imine/hemiaminal** | Central C\u2013N intermediate; reduction gives glycine or disproportionation gives glycine + oxamate | Metal reductive amination and aza-Cannizzaro. |\n| **N-oxalylglycine** | Bound glycine precursor/reservoir | Acidic glyoxylate\u2013NH\u2083 chemistry. |\n| **Hydantoin / N-carbamoylglycine** | Protected glycine skeleton preceding hydrolysis/decarboxylation | Bucherer\u2013Bergs ketoacid/cyanide chemistry. |\n| **Hydroxylamine/formaldoxime** | Alternative fixed-N entry; generates HCN/NH\u2083 and nitrile chemistry | H\u2082CO/NH\u2082OH route. |\n| **Aminoacetaldehyde** | Oxidation intermediate | Ethanolamine route. |\n| **Ethylene glycol** | C\u2082 precursor oxidized photocatalytically to glyoxylate | Methanol/nitrate/TiO\u2082 route. |\n| **Formate/formic acid** | Impact precursor; proton shuttle/reductant in some theoretical networks | Shock chemistry, CO reduction, Cannizzaro chemistry. |\n\nThe corresponding catalyst map is equally heterogeneous. **No mineral catalyst is intrinsically needed** for classical Strecker formation once H\u2082CO/HCN/NH\u2083 are present, HCN condensation, formaldehyde/ammonia thermal chemistry, or concentrated glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro chemistry.\n\n**Fe-containing minerals/metals** have the broadest experimental coverage: FeS/Fe\u2070 promotes reductive amination; Fe-bearing brucite couples nitrate reduction to glycine synthesis; Fe metal is active in ethanolamine oxidation; and hydrothermal sulfide/oxide assemblages can buffer redox conditions during Strecker chemistry. Whether these solids act catalytically or stoichiometrically must be specified\u2014Fe(II) oxidation in brucite and FeS/Fe\u2070 systems consumes reducing equivalents and therefore is not simply heterogeneous catalysis in the conventional sense.\n\n**Ni** is particularly effective when molecular H\u2082 is the terminal reductant, including supported Ni and Ni-rich meteoritic material. **Co, Fe, Cu and Ni metal** facilitate ethanolamine-to-glycine oxidation, with Co strongest in the published alkaline system. **Anatase TiO\u2082**, especially with Ba\u00b2\u207a surface modification, enables the methanol/nitrate photochemical cascade. **Cu cyanide complexes** enable visible/UV photoredox electron chemistry in cyanosulfidic networks. **Molybdate and transition-metal ions** modify product selectivity in hydroxylamine/formaldehyde seawater simulations rather than acting as uniquely required glycine catalysts.\n\n## Environmental plausibility and comparative assessment\n\nThere is no evidence-based basis for assigning glycine synthesis uniquely to \u201chydrothermal vents,\u201d \u201cwarm little ponds,\u201d \u201cthe atmosphere,\u201d or \u201cmeteorites.\u201d The experimentally supported picture is pluralistic.\n\n**Surface atmosphere\u2013water systems have the strongest evidence for generating the immediate Strecker feedstocks from genuinely simple gases.** Electric discharge converts CH\u2084/N\u2082/NH\u2083/H\u2082O or sufficiently H\u2082-rich CO/CO\u2082 mixtures into HCN, formaldehyde and amino-acid products without the experimenter first supplying an activated C\u2082 substrate. Their weakness is atmospheric redox. Neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 chemistry is inefficient, while sufficiently reducing global atmospheres remain geologically disputed. Local volcanic plumes and transient post-impact H\u2082/CH\u2084 atmospheres substantially weaken\u2014but do not eliminate\u2014that criticism.\n\n**Concentrating surface environments are especially attractive for subsequent aqueous chemistry.** Classical Strecker, HCN polymerization, aza-Cannizzaro and much cyanosulfidic chemistry all improve when reactants reach millimolar-to-molar concentrations. Evaporation, freezing and adsorption are therefore not incidental details but likely determine whether an atmospheric feedstock flux becomes chemically consequential. The Levy\u2013Miller freezing experiments establish eutectic ice as one experimentally demonstrated way to concentrate cyanide chemistry; the Jarois aza-Cannizzaro data independently show why concentration matters for glyoxylate.\n\n**Alkaline hydrothermal systems now have stronger glycine-specific experimental support than they did in the 1990s.** The important progress is not high-temperature Strecker synthesis but mild-temperature glyoxylate reductive amination using FeS/Fe\u2070, ferroan brucite or H\u2082/Ni. These reactions operate near room temperature to modest temperature and make direct use of reducing power characteristic of serpentinizing systems. Their common unresolved variable is upstream substrate supply: neither glyoxylate nor tens-of-millimolar fixed nitrogen should simply be assumed.\n\n**High-temperature vent interiors are less favorable for maintaining a free-glycine inventory.** They can synthesize precursors and support rapid reaction chemistry, but amino acids also dimerize, cyclize and decompose as temperature increases. Thus vent models are most chemically plausible when they invoke spatial gradients\u2014hot synthesis regions coupled to cooler mixing zones, porous mineral walls or circulation\u2014rather than treating a homogeneous 300\u00b0C reactor as the origin environment.\n\n**Impact chemistry is plausible but probably episodic.** It can reduce N\u2082, generate reduced gases and HCN in impact plumes, or produce glycine directly from ammonium formate/bicarbonate and shocked ices. Direct recovered glycine yields in simple aqueous shock experiments are low, and the balance between synthesis and thermal destruction is sensitive to the pressure\u2013temperature\u2013time trajectory.\n\n**Extraterrestrial synthesis is experimentally very strong as a source term**, because independent UV-ice experiments and meteorite-parent-body studies converge on glycine formation. It does not compete logically with terrestrial routes: delivered glycine, HCN, aldehydes and related precursors could supplement atmospheric or hydrothermal inventories. The central uncertainties are the delivery flux of intact material, atmospheric-entry survival, impact destruction, and dilution after deposition.\n\nA useful overall assessment is therefore:\n\n| Question | Route with strongest present experimental answer | Main unresolved issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Can glycine be made from very simple atmospheric feedstocks? | Spark discharge and energetic-particle chemistry | Planetary redox state and natural energy flux. |\n| Is there a robust, chemically understood solution route? | Classical Strecker | HCN/H\u2082CO concentrations and nitrile hydrolysis. |\n| Can glycine form without cyanide? | Glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro; Fe/Ni reductive amination; nitrate\u2013TiO\u2082; ethanolamine oxidation | Production/concentration of glyoxylate or other immediate precursor. |\n| Can fixed nitrogen be generated in the same system? | Ferroan brucite NO\u2083\u207b reduction; TiO\u2082 NO\u2083\u207b photoreduction; high-energy N\u2082 chemistry | Plausible nitrate/N\u2082 inventory and conversion rate. |\n| Can H\u2082 drive metabolism-like amino-acid synthesis? | Ni/H\u2082 and reduced Fe mineral systems | Natural catalyst state and ketoacid availability. |\n| Can concentration occur without evaporation? | Freeze-concentrated cyanide chemistry | Requires suitable cold regions and long residence times. |\n| Is exogenous glycine formation demonstrable? | UV-irradiated interstellar ice and parent-body Strecker chemistry | Quantitative transfer to usable terrestrial inventories. |\n\n## Open questions, critiques, and priorities for synthesis-network design\n\nThe first unresolved issue is **feedstock closure**. Many high-yield glycine experiments begin one or two network levels too late. A 59% conversion of supplied glyoxylate to glycine is chemically impressive, but an origin scenario must also show where the glyoxylate came from. A hydrothermal Strecker experiment with ~0.2 M HCN and formaldehyde demonstrates reaction compatibility at 150\u00b0C, not endogenous production of those concentrations. A 23% ethanolamine conversion similarly transfers the upstream problem to ethanolamine. For network evaluation, the relevant metric should therefore be flux from geochemically defensible starting materials\u2014CO\u2082, CO, CH\u2084, N\u2082, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, H\u2082, H\u2082S, nitrate/nitrite and mineral reductants\u2014not merely isolated-step yield.\n\nThe second is **concentration**. Classical cyanide chemistry can become effective near 10 mM or above; aza-Cannizzaro glycine production becomes weak below roughly 0.25 M glyoxylate on short timescales; the nitrate/brucite work used ~0.1 M reactants; TiO\u2082 photocatalysis used ~0.4 M methanol and ~0.1 M nitrate; the ethanolamine route strongly deteriorated below 0.1 M. These are not fatal concentrations in an evaporating or freezing microenvironment, but they are inconsistent with an unconcentrated global ocean. A credible model therefore needs a hydrological concentrating step.\n\nThe third is **analytical definition of \u201cglycine synthesis.\u201d** Some of the most frequently cited atmospheric, particle-irradiation, hydrothermal and impact experiments quantify amino acids only after 6 M HCl hydrolysis or comparable work-up. Those experiments demonstrate formation of a **glycine-generating precursor inventory**, which may actually be advantageous because bound material can be more persistent than free amino acids, but it should not be entered into a chemical network as direct free-glycine production.\n\nThe fourth is **reaction compatibility**. Cyanide is beneficial for Strecker/Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry but binds transition metals and can interfere with redox chemistry. Sulfide is a reductant and photochemical electron donor but precipitates many transition metals. Strongly alkaline conditions help certain reductive-amination or Cannizzaro processes but alter HCN/CN\u207b partitioning and metal solubility. High temperature accelerates nitrile hydrolysis and carbonyl reactions but also destroys amino acids. Thus, assembling individually successful reactions into one \u201cone-pot\u201d pathway can be chemically misleading; spatially or temporally separated environments may be required. The demonstrated interference of cyanide with Fe(II)-based nitrogen reduction and the temperature sensitivity of FeS reduction chemistry exemplify this general problem.\n\nThe fifth is **catalyst geological realism**. Fresh FeS, ferroan brucite and common oxides are easier to motivate geochemically than Pd/C or engineered Ba\u00b2\u207a-TiO\u2082 nanoparticles, but even a plausible mineral identity does not guarantee the oxidation state, crystal defects, surface area or lifetime used experimentally. The 2019 FeS/Fe\u2070 system required electrochemical pretreatment to achieve especially active material; the 2024 TiO\u2082 system used a deliberately optimized photocatalyst; the Ni/H\u2082 work shows excellent chemistry but still needs a natural route to sufficiently reactive Ni surfaces. Catalyst availability must therefore be represented as a mineral-formation subnetwork, not a binary \u201cmineral present/mineral absent\u201d variable.\n\nThe sixth is the unresolved **cyanide-versus-metabolic-skeleton debate**. Classical Strecker chemistry has the deepest experimental pedigree and naturally explains why glycine is so readily formed, but extant amino-acid biosynthesis uses ketoacid reductive amination/transamination instead. Pulletikurti and colleagues explicitly targeted this discontinuity with ketoacid/Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry, while Kaur and colleagues showed that H\u2082/Ni can perform genuinely cyanide-free, metabolism-like reductive amination. No evolutionary argument yet shows that one must exclude the other: early cyanide chemistry could have supplied amino acids before later network takeover by ketoacid chemistry, or ketoacid chemistry may have operated contemporaneously in different environments.\n\nThe seventh is **atmospheric uncertainty**. The traditional criticism of Miller chemistry\u2014that an enduring CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere conflicts with an oxidized mantle\u2014is supported by zircon/mantle-redox interpretations, but impact models predict transient H\u2082/CH\u2084-rich intervals, and a 2026 thermodynamic analysis has revived the stronger claim that late-Hadean conditions could have supported an H\u2082/CH\u2084/NH\u2083-rich atmosphere. These models invoke different assumptions about mantle redox evolution, atmosphere\u2013ocean\u2013crust exchange and impact history. Consequently, network calculations should test several atmospheric boundary conditions instead of hard-coding either a permanently reducing or permanently CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere.\n\nFinally, there remains a **kinetics-versus-thermodynamics problem**. Computational reaction-network searches can formally connect CO, H\u2082O and NH\u2083 to glycine through formic acid, formamide, aminomethanol and formaldimine, but calculated free-energy barriers in one recent study are commonly **~40\u201360+ kcal mol\u207b\u00b9**, including a ~51 kcal mol\u207b\u00b9 effective bottleneck for one glycine pathway and ~63 kcal mol\u207b\u00b9 for another CO/H\u2082/H\u2082O/NH\u2083 path. Such networks are useful for discovering possible connectivity but, absent a catalyst or high-energy event that radically lowers those barriers, they should not be treated as experimentally viable ambient-temperature prebiotic syntheses.\n\nTaken together, the literature supports a network-level conclusion rather than a single-route conclusion: **glycine itself is not a particularly difficult molecule to make abiotically once either (a) formaldehyde + cyanide + reduced nitrogen or (b) glyoxylate + a nitrogen source + reducing equivalents are available. The difficult prebiotic questions lie upstream\u2014fixed nitrogen, C\u2081/C\u2082 carbonyl production, concentration, mineral redox state\u2014and downstream\u2014hydrolysis, preservation, transport, and integration with peptide/nucleotide chemistry.** The highest-priority discriminating experiments are therefore not additional demonstrations that concentrated laboratory feedstocks can yield glycine, but closed-system experiments starting from geochemically constrained C/N sources, using natural mineral assemblages, realistic concentration cycles and analytically resolving free glycine from its hydrolyzable precursor pool.\n\n## Selected primary literature\n\n**Atmospheric and cyanide chemistry.** Stanley L. Miller, 1953, \u201cA Production of Amino Acids Under Possible Primitive Earth Conditions,\u201d *Science*. Stanley L. Miller, 1957, \u201cThe Mechanism of Synthesis of Amino Acids by Electric Discharges,\u201d *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*. G. Schlesinger and Stanley L. Miller, 1983, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis in Atmospheres Containing CH\u2084, CO, and CO\u2082. I. Amino Acids,\u201d *Journal of Molecular Evolution*. Adam P. Johnson, H. James Cleaves, Jason P. Dworkin, Daniel P. Glavin, Antonio Lazcano and Jeffrey L. Bada, 2008, \u201cThe Miller Volcanic Spark Discharge Experiment,\u201d *Science*. Eric T. Parker and colleagues, 2011, \u201cPrimordial Synthesis of Amines and Amino Acids in a 1958 Miller H\u2082S-rich Spark Discharge Experiment,\u201d *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*. Juan Or\u00f3 and S. S. Kamat, 1961, \u201cAmino-acid Synthesis from Hydrogen Cyanide under Possible Primitive Earth Conditions,\u201d *Nature*.\n\n**Nitrogen/carbonyl routes.** Juan Or\u00f3, A. P. Kimball, S. Fritz and F. Master, 1959, \u201cAmino Acid Synthesis from Formaldehyde and Hydroxylamine,\u201d *Archives of Biochemistry and Biophysics*. Sidney W. Fox and C. R. Windsor, 1970, \u201cSynthesis of Amino Acids by the Heating of Formaldehyde and Ammonia,\u201d *Science*. Hiroshi Yanagawa, Y. Makino, K. Sato, M. Nishizawa and F. Egami, 1984, \u201cNovel Formation of Alpha-amino Acids from Alpha-oxo Acids and Ammonia in an Aqueous Medium,\u201d *Origins of Life*.\n\n**Glyoxylate and mineral routes.** Norio Kitadai, Ryuhei Nakamura, Masahiro Yamamoto, Ken Takai, Naohiro Yoshida and Yoshi Oono, 2019, \u201cMetals Likely Promoted Protometabolism in Early Ocean Alkaline Hydrothermal Systems,\u201d *Science Advances*. Sunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen and Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy, 2022, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of \u03b1-Amino Acids and Orotate from \u03b1-Ketoacids Potentiates Transition to Extant Metabolic Pathways,\u201d *Nature Chemistry*. Harpreet Kaur, Sophia A. Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William F. Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz and Joseph Moran, 2024, \u201cA Prebiotic Krebs Cycle Analog Generates Amino Acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over Nickel,\u201d *Chem*. Lucy Chimiak and colleagues, 2024, \u201cGlycine Synthesis from Nitrate and Glyoxylate Mediated by Ferroan Brucite,\u201d *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*. Dean R. Jarois, Lars E. Schimmelpfennig and Samuel H. Gellman, 2024, \u201cA New Mechanism for Formation of Glycine from Glyoxylic Acid: the Aza-Cannizzaro Reaction,\u201d *Chemistry\u2014A European Journal*.\n\n**Photochemical and high-energy chemistry.** Bhavesh H. Patel, Claudia Percivalle, Dougal J. Ritson, Colm D. Duffy and John D. Sutherland, 2015, \u201cCommon Origins of RNA, Protein and Lipid Precursors in a Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism,\u201d *Nature Chemistry*. Peifeng Li, Wanghui Zhao, Kaixuan Wang, Tao Wang and Biaobiao Zhang, 2024, \u201cPhotocatalytic Synthesis of Glycine from Methanol and Nitrate,\u201d *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*. Kensei Kobayashi and collaborators, 1998, \u201cAmino Acid Formation in Gas Mixtures by High Energy Particle Irradiation,\u201d *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*.\n\n**Hydrothermal and impact routes.** R. J.-C. Hennet, Nils G. Holm and Michael H. Engel, 1992, \u201cAbiotic Synthesis of Amino Acids under Hydrothermal Conditions and the Origin of Life,\u201d *Naturwissenschaften*. Xianlong Zhang, Ge Tian, Jing Gao, Mei Han, Rui Su, Yanxiang Wang and Shouhua Feng, 2017, ethanolamine-to-glycine hydrothermal experiments, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*. Chizuka Suzuki, Yoshihiro Furukawa, Takamichi Kobayashi, Toshimori Sekine, Hiromoto Nakazawa and Takeshi Kakegawa, 2015, \u201cShock Wave Synthesis of Amino Acids from Solutions of Ammonium Formate and Ammonium Bicarbonate,\u201d *Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems*. Arthur L. Weber, 1998, \u201cPrebiotic Amino Acid Thioester Synthesis: Thiol-Dependent Amino Acid Synthesis from Formose Substrates,\u201d *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*.\n\n**Cosmochemical routes.** Max P. Bernstein, Jason P. Dworkin, Scott A. Sandford, George W. Cooper and Louis J. Allamandola, 2002, \u201cRacemic Amino Acids from the Ultraviolet Photolysis of Interstellar Ice Analogues,\u201d *Nature*. Guillermo M. Mu\u00f1oz Caro and colleagues, 2002, \u201cAmino Acids from Ultraviolet Irradiation of Interstellar Ice Analogues,\u201d *Nature*. Chang-Woo Lee, Joon-Ki Kim, Eui-Seong Moon, Y. C. Minh and Heon Kang, 2009, glycine formation in UV-irradiated interstellar-ice analogues, *The Astrophysical Journal*. N. R. Lerner, E. Peterson and S. Chang, 1993, \u201cThe Strecker Synthesis as a Source of Amino Acids in Carbonaceous Chondrites,\u201d *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.84\n- Feedback: Network-level assessment: validity 0.98, chemical feasibility 0.83, environmental consistency 0.64, pathway coherence 0.87, target achievement 0.90, and literature alignment 0.82. The 24 molecule nodes and 18 reaction nodes form a connected network with consistent IDs and sufficient intermediate resolution. The deterministic check establishes that all reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients; there are no conservation violations. Explicitly store the inferred coefficients for rxn_009, rxn_011, rxn_012 and rxn_017. The network contains credible routes to free glycine, particularly the Strecker, aza-Cannizzaro and reductive-amination chemistry. Conditional reservoir-release and speculative mechanistic branches do not invalidate that supported core, but they are not equally established target-producing routes. Declared external feedstocks are accepted as boundary conditions rather than topology failures; their concentrations and delivery still require quantitative environmental justification. The main network-wide weakness is that Surface, Hydrothermal and Biochemical labels are not accompanied by numerical conditions or physically specified transfer processes. Cyanide/metal separation and cooling are sensible compatibility measures, but their implementation remains undescribed. Catalyst states, thiol participation, hydrolysis kinetics and product preservation need explicit treatment. Literature correspondence is substantial and important critiques are acknowledged, but reaction-specific citations and experimental conditions are absent from the submitted entries. Overall confidence is below the 0.90 network approval threshold, so the complete network is not APPROVED. Individual pathway approval flags use the separately requested 0.80 threshold.\n- Issues: ['Evaluated as oxime formation and cyanide generation followed by the P1 Strecker sequence. Or\u00f3 et al. (1959) and Kamaluddin et al. (1979) support glycine production and the appearance of cyanide, aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide in formaldehyde/hydroxylamine mixtures. That evidence does not independently establish rxn_008 as a sufficiently productive isolated aqueous oxime-dehydration step. The encoded pathway coherently uses separately supplied ammonia; rxn_008 itself does not produce ammonia. Hydroxylamine supply is appropriately acknowledged as a boundary assumption.', 'N-oxalylglycine formation agrees with Yanagawa et al. (1984), and rxn_012 balances with two glyoxylic acid equivalents. The central limitation is liberation of free glycine: the cited yields followed approximately 6 M HCl hydrolysis, not demonstrated release under the precursor-forming aqueous conditions. Amide hydrolysis is chemically legitimate, but its environmental rate and selectivity are unestablished here. The network appropriately labels rxn_013 conditional; that caveat prevents overclaiming but does not validate target release.', 'Both equations conserve atoms, and Pulletikurti et al. (2022) supports cyanide-assisted ketoacid chemistry involving hydantoin-family and N-carbamoylglycine products. However, rxn_014 is a lumped transformation whose particular mechanism and reagent requirements are not documented in the reaction entry. The supplied literature supports heated processing, not unrestricted surface conversion and decarbamoylation. Missing explicit hydantoin nodes are not inherently invalid, but the lumped representation needs experimental justification and an established route to free glycine.', 'Zhang et al. (2017) supports metal-assisted hydrothermal ethanolamine-to-glycine conversion, including activity with iron, but not definitive validation of this exact two-step mechanism. With rxn_016 used twice and the inferred rxn_017 coefficients, the net conversion is ethanolamine + water -> glycine + 2 H2; no external oxidant is required by conservation. Nevertheless, hydrogen evolution must be established, and aminoacetaldehyde disproportionation competes with condensation chemistry because this intermediate is unstable and enolizable. The speculative labeling is appropriate.', 'The balanced net equation permits internal redox conversion without an external oxidant. Weber (1998) supports low-yield glycine formation from glyoxal in thiol-containing mixtures, so this branch has experimental precedent. However, the thiol identity, loading, availability and fate are absent from the submitted environment description. The proposed thioester chemistry also makes it important to distinguish free glycine from a bound precursor pool. The reported approximately 0.41% yield demonstrates formation but does not establish substantial network flux.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.88\n- Feedback: The network has sufficient nodes, consistent molecular identities, and a connected reaction graph. The deterministic conservation check is accepted: eight reactions are balanced as listed and seven are balanceable with reasonable coefficients; none violates atom or charge conservation. Atmospheric reactions are acceptable lumped precursor-supply equations, not elementary mechanisms or quantitative yield predictions. Literature alignment is strong for the Strecker, aza-Cannizzaro, FeS-Fe0, H2/Ni, and ferroan-brucite systems. Several supported branches genuinely produce glycine, and the explicitly conditional hydroxylamine branch does not negate those routes. Declared feedstock and mineral inventories are accepted as boundary resources, without assuming upstream closure or mineral regeneration. Nevertheless, the supplied reaction entries lack the numerical operating conditions, reaction-level citations, and physical transfer specifications needed to verify the complete implementation. Hydrolysis kinetics and preservation, active mineral preparation, and dissolved-versus-mineral-associated product accounting remain important gaps. P3 and P4 were evaluated through the imine-first branch because their reaction sequences were not explicitly enumerated. Flagged reactions require additional substantiation or condition specification; none is flagged for conservation failure. Route count, shared hubs, and environmental ordering were not scoring advantages or disadvantages. The network is credible but does not meet the 0.90 network-approval threshold as submitted.\n- Issues: ['The balanced ternary equation is chemically conceivable through oxime formation, cyanide generation, and Strecker capture, but balance does not demonstrate that this exact formulation works. Oro et al. (1959) and Kamaluddin et al. (1979) support related formaldehyde/hydroxylamine chemistry and observed nitrile/amide intermediates, not necessarily the proposed ammonia-supplemented recipe. The conditional designation is appropriate. Its three-step count begins at the formaldehyde hub rather than at a listed boundary feedstock.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"A connected network of 24 chemical-species nodes and 15 reaction nodes defines ten distinct routes to glycine. Six routes combine three experimentally supported atmospheric feedstock inventories with two Strecker entry branches: transient methanimine formation or passage through a glycolonitrile reservoir. These are distinct network pathways, not six independent reaction families. Four cyanide-free routes use glyoxylate through aza-Cannizzaro chemistry, H2/Ni reduction, reduced FeS/Fe0 chemistry, or nitrate/ferroan-brucite chemistry. Glyoxylate supply and concentration are explicit boundary assumptions, not solved upstream synthesis. Atmospheric reactions are lumped precursor-supply transformations rather than elementary reactions or yield predictions. Reaction-specific numerical design assumptions are distinguished from reported experimental conditions. Surface Strecker mixtures undergo advective transfer into moderately hot, pressurized fractures for hydrolysis, followed by cooling; the mineral-reduction branches occupy separate, cyanide-free compartments. Biochemical denotes nonenzymatic mineral-interface chemistry and permits the near-room-temperature conditions actually studied. Neutral organic formulas and explicit proton equivalents provide reference-state bookkeeping; actual protonation, hydration, adsorption and metal speciation depend on pH. environment_formed records one representative setting, not an exclusive location. Mineral resources are finite boundary inventories: neither their natural formation nor their regeneration is assumed. The restricted role vocabulary requires terminal coproducts to be labeled intermediate.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dinitrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"nitrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/N2/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-iminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxamic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"zero-valent iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton equivalent\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"NO3-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"nitrate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"nitrate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeMg2H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) dimagnesium hexahydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferroan brucite, idealized Fe:Mg ratio 1:2\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeHO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(III) oxide hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferric oxyhydroxide, modeled oxidation sink\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"MgH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"magnesium hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"brucite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Methane-ammonia discharge supplies Strecker precursors\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K bulk gas-water system; transient discharge channels are much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 0.7-1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic gas-water interface with repeated electrical discharge and condensation. Johnson's reconstructed volcanic experiment used initial CH4:NH3:H2 partial pressures of 200:200:100 torr and approximately one week of sparking. Bulk temperature here is a design range. Hydrogen may be present initially although it is a product in the formal net equation. Rainout or condensate drainage collects soluble precursors; subsequent concentration requires sheltered, partially evaporating pools rather than a dilute ocean.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electrical fragmentation and radical recombination generate carbonyl and cyanide species, which dissolve in water. Balanced precursor bookkeeping: 2 CH4 + NH3 + H2O -> CH2O + HCN + 5 H2. This equation is a selected-product projection, not an elementary step, fixed product distribution or conversion yield.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Discharge production of glycine precursors and downstream Strecker chemistry is supported by Miller (1953, 1957), with experimentally productive volcanic apparatus configurations documented by Johnson et al. (2008). A persistent global methane-ammonia atmosphere is not assumed: the plausible boundary is a transient reducing atmosphere or localized reduced plume. Ammonia photolysis, precursor dilution and uncertain gas replenishment limit environmental productivity. No mineral is required for this precursor-generation experiment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-rich carbon-monoxide-nitrogen discharge supplies Strecker precursors\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K bulk collection system; nonthermal electrical excitation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Illustrative design boundary: dry-gas H2:CO:N2 ratio 8:1:1, equilibrated with liquid water and repeatedly discharged. This ratio is not presented as an exact published recipe. Operate without O2; trap products in water and concentrate them in partially enclosed shoreline pools. Sustained hydrogen-rich gas supply is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Discharge activates CO and N2; hydrogen-bearing radicals support formation of HCN, ammonia and formaldehyde. Balanced selected-product net: 2 CO + N2 + 4 H2 -> HCN + CH2O + NH3 + H2O. Water is physically present even though its formal net coefficient is on the product side.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Schlesinger and Miller (1983) demonstrated strong redox dependence of amino-acid and precursor production in CO-containing atmospheres, with improved HCN and formaldehyde production under sufficiently hydrogen-rich conditions. The equation represents a lumped inventory-producing network, not thermal reduction of N2 by H2 at 300 K. The stated gas ratio is a testable design assumption; its planetary persistence and product collection efficiency are unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-rich carbon-dioxide-nitrogen discharge supplies Strecker precursors\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K bulk collection system; transient energetic discharge\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Illustrative design boundary: dry-gas H2:CO2:N2 ratio 8:1:1 over liquid water, with repeated discharge and aqueous trapping. This is a proposed hydrogen-rich operating inventory, not an exact published recipe. A neutral CO2-N2 atmosphere without substantial H2 is explicitly excluded from the productive scenario. Concentration occurs after collection in shaded or sediment-covered aqueous pockets.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Energetic activation, reduction and C-N coupling produce a mixed precursor inventory. Balanced selected-product net: 2 CO2 + N2 + 6 H2 -> HCN + CH2O + NH3 + 3 H2O. The equation does not imply selective or spontaneous ambient-temperature CO2 fixation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Schlesinger and Miller (1983) support precursor and glycine production from sufficiently reducing CO2-containing gas systems and show sharply declining productivity as hydrogen availability falls. This branch has a larger reduction demand than the CO branch. Transient hydrogen-rich atmospheric conditions are a boundary assumption, not a settled Hadean atmospheric model, and gas-to-usable-precursor flux remains unquantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formaldehyde ammoniation to methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed concentrated-pool window: pH 8-9.5, approximately 10-100 mM total formaldehyde and 0.1-0.3 M total ammonia plus ammonium. These concentration ranges are design assumptions, not attributed to one experiment. Use covered porewater or shallow sediment to reduce UV destruction. Methanimine is a transient equilibrium species, not an isolated bulk feedstock.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to formaldehyde through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration: CH2O + NH3 <-> CH2NH + H2O. Subsequent cyanide trapping can pull this unfavorable aqueous condensation forward.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The imine-first Strecker mechanism is consistent with Miller (1957) and the experimentally established aldehyde-ammonia-cyanide chemistry summarized by Hennet et al. (1992). Formaldehyde hydration and imine hydrolysis compete strongly in water, so the node does not assert quantitative methanimine accumulation. Evaporation must concentrate reactants without assuming that volatile HCN and ammonia are retained perfectly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same aqueous compartment as rxn_004; proposed pH 8-9.5 and 10-100 mM total cyanide. HCN/CN- and imine/iminium speciation supplies the reactive pair. No Ni, Cu or Fe0 catalyst is introduced into this cyanide-rich branch.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer: CH2NH + HCN -> NH2CH2CN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile formation as the central link between discharge-generated aldehydes/cyanide and amino acids. The two-carbon glycine skeleton is assembled here without a mineral catalyst. Competing cyanide polymerization, cyanohydrin formation and dilution constrain the fraction entering aminoacetonitrile; the graph does not assign a universal yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-9.5 and millimolar-to-0.1 M formaldehyde/cyanide in collected condensate. Partial evaporation or restricted porewater exchange increases concentration. UV-exposed production zones drain into shaded reaction reservoirs.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CN- adds reversibly to formaldehyde and the alkoxide is protonated: CH2O + HCN <-> HOCH2CN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formaldehyde-cyanide capture and glycolonitrile participation are supported by the Strecker framework of Miller (1957) and the cyanohydrin-centered chemistry used by Patel et al. (2015). No copper catalyst, sulfide donor or UV photoredox step is required for this particular addition. Glycolonitrile provides an alternative entry reservoir, not evidence for an independently complete cyanosulfidic pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Net ammoniation of the glycolonitrile reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich aqueous reservoir, proposed pH 8-10 and 0.1-0.3 M total ammonia plus ammonium. The actual conversion time and cyanohydrin/aminonitrile distribution require measurement. Retain the mixture in covered pores rather than treating drying to completion as necessary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped reversible cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion: HOCH2CN + NH3 <-> NH2CH2CN + H2O. This can proceed through cyanohydrin dissociation, carbonyl ammoniation and cyanide recapture; it is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) and Patel et al. (2015) support the interconnected cyanohydrin/aminonitrile chemistry underlying this branch. Its distinction from rxn_004-rxn_005 is passage through an accumulated glycolonitrile reservoir, not necessarily a different elementary C-N bond-forming mechanism. Ammonia supply, equilibrium competition and possible cyanohydrin hydrolysis remain limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity providing acid catalysis; no mineral catalyst required\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 2-4 in a moderately hot fracture, not a black-smoker core. Aminonitrile-containing surface water enters coastal subseafloor fractures by advective groundwater exchange. A 6-48 h total hot-zone residence is a screening window shared with rxn_009, not an established conversion time. Unreacted cyanide is not assumed to disappear quantitatively, and this flow path does not enter the Ni/Fe reduction compartments.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile, proton transfers and tautomerization yield the amide: NH2CH2CN + H2O -> NH2CH2CONH2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kamaluddin et al. (1979) observed aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide before amino-acid accumulation. Hennet et al. (1992) demonstrated overall Strecker-compatible glycine synthesis around 423 K and approximately 10 bar. These studies support the chemistry but do not establish isolated-step kinetics for the proposed pH/residence window. Nitrile hydrolysis is therefore retained as a kinetic bottleneck rather than treated as instantaneous.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis and preservation of free glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity providing acid catalysis; no mineral catalyst required\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K during hydrolysis; below 330 K after outflow cooling\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar during hydrolysis\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 2-4 during the same finite hot-zone residence as rxn_008. Outflow mixes with approximately 5-10 volumes of cooler anoxic water and contacts carbonate-buffered sediments, targeting below 330 K and pH 6-8 within minutes to hours. These are physical design targets, not measured natural residence times. Cooling dilutes the product and does not itself solve subsequent concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the amide carbonyl and C-N cleavage releases glycine and ammonia: NH2CH2CONH2 + H2O -> NH2CH2COOH + NH3. Actual products are protonated or zwitterionic according to pH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The glycinamide-to-glycine sequence is supported by Kamaluddin et al. (1979), while Hennet et al. (1992) supports glycine formation during hydrothermal Strecker processing. This is ordinary amide hydrolysis, not release from an unidentified polymer inferred solely after 6 M HCl work-up. Nevertheless, conversion under the proposed milder acidity must be measured. Finite heating and explicit cooling address competing cyclization, oligomerization and decomposition; quantitative survival is not assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-ammonia condensation to a reducible imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-303 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, or 5 bar H2 when coupled to rxn_011\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate cyanide-free mineral-interface compartments. Use the downstream branch's pH: approximately 8 for Ni/H2, 9.6 for reduced FeS/Fe0, or alkaline conditions for ferroan brucite. Initial glyoxylate is millimolar to 0.1 M depending on the cited system. Ammonia may be supplied initially or generated in situ by rxn_014. The imine is formed and consumed in situ, not transferred as an isolated stock solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible ammonia addition to glyoxylate through a hemiaminal, followed by dehydration: OHC-COOH + NH3 <-> HN=CH-COOH + H2O. Reduction can pull the equilibrium toward product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination of glyoxylate is supported by Kitadai et al. (2019), Kaur et al. (2024) and Chimiak et al. (2024). The imine is a conventional mechanistic representation, not a claim that every study isolated or quantified it. Glyoxylate hydration and limited imine abundance compete with reduction. The origin and concentration of the supplied C2 substrate remain explicit feedstock-closure gaps.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of glyoxylate imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Reactive nickel-bearing metallic surface, including the experimentally tested meteoritic analogue\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-bearing metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Representative reported regime: approximately 30 mM initial ketoacid, pH 8, ammonium bicarbonate-containing water and 72 h reaction time. The concentration refers to starting glyoxylate equivalents, not isolated imine. A cool, gas-charged subsurface mineral interface can maintain pressure. Exclude cyanide and strongly sulfide-rich inflow to limit metal poisoning. Reactive Ni surface availability is a boundary assumption.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel dissociates H2 and transfers reducing equivalents to the C=N bond: HN=CH-COOH + H2 -> NH2CH2COOH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven ketoacid reductive amination with Ni-containing catalysts and Ni-bearing meteorite material; metal-free controls did not produce the corresponding reduction products. This branch uses H2 as the consumed reductant and Ni as catalyst, unlike the consumable iron systems. The reported 6-84% range spans multiple substrates and catalyst conditions and is not assigned here as a glycine-specific yield. Natural Ni abundance, accessible surface area, poisoning and competing glyoxylate reduction to glycolate limit extrapolation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduction of glyoxylate imine by reduced FeS-Fe0 material\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Electrochemically pre-reduced FeS-Fe0 assemblage; FeS is the interfacial matrix and Fe0 is explicitly consumed\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in the laboratory analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS interfacial matrix; zero-valent iron is a stoichiometric reductant, not a regenerated catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported organic-reaction regime: pH approximately 9.6 and 48 h, with millimolar glyoxylate and excess ammonium. Active material was prepared at potentials at or below approximately -0.7 V versus SHE. That preparation is a boundary condition, not an assumed spontaneous mineral-formation step. Explicit H+ denotes buffered proton equivalents, not addition of strong acid to the alkaline suspension. Fe2+ is reference-state redox bookkeeping and need not accumulate as free aquo ion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Interfacial electron transfer reduces the imine while low-valence iron is oxidized. Balanced reference-state net: HN=CH-COOH + Fe0 + 2 H+ -> NH2CH2COOH + Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) reported approximately 59% glycine under the active reduced-FeS conditions. This supports glyoxylate reductive amination, but the Fe0-to-FeII equation is a simplified electron-source accounting model, not a complete surface mechanism or phase-speciation measurement. FeII can complex, adsorb or precipitate at the stated pH. Natural formation and persistence of comparably active FeS-Fe0 material, its finite electron capacity and passivation remain unresolved; no iron regeneration or universal catalytic turnover is implied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Buffered glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro disproportionation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Phosphate buffer supporting proton transfer; no metal catalyst or external reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported regime: approximately 0.5 M glyoxylic acid and 0.5 M ammonium phosphate, pH 7-10, for 48 h. Use a strongly concentrated evaporitic porewater or covered pool margin. Below roughly 0.25 M glyoxylate, little reaction occurred on the studied timescale. Buffer is chemically important; unbuffered glyoxylate plus stoichiometric NH4OH is not treated as equivalent.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate-derived imine/hemiaminal species exchange a hydride: one carbon center is reduced to glycine and the other is oxidized to oxamate. Balanced neutral-reference net: 2 OHC-COOH + 2 NH3 -> NH2CH2COOH + NH2COCOOH + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Jarois et al. (2024) reported approximately 11-14% glycine near pH 7-10 under the concentrated, buffered 323 K conditions, with isotopic evidence supporting glyoxylate-derived hydride transfer. This is a supported cyanide-free route without an external redox reagent. Its dominant limitation is achieving and maintaining approximately molar-scale combined solute inventories; ordinary dilute seawater is not a credible substitute. Phosphate functions as part of the proton-transfer environment rather than an assumed generic mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferroan-brucite-mediated nitrate reduction supplies ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ferroan brucite surface; structural Fe(II) is a finite electron donor\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported boundary concentrations are approximately 100 mM nitrate and 100 mM glyoxylate, with incubation for six weeks. Use an anoxic alkaline mineral slurry; pH 9-11 is a proposed operating bracket rather than an exact reported recipe. Reduction occurs alongside organic chemistry in the same cyanide-free suspension, not necessarily as a completed nitrate pretreatment. H+ is a formal buffered proton equivalent, not bulk acidification.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Structural Fe(II) supplies the eight electrons needed to reduce nitrate to ammonia. Let M denote the idealized formula FeMg2H6O6. An atom- and charge-balanced mineral-endpoint model is: NO3- + 8 M + H+ -> NH3 + 8 FeOOH + 16 Mg(OH)2 + 3 H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chimiak et al. (2024) demonstrated coupled nitrate/glyoxylate chemistry with ferroan brucite and attributed fixed-nitrogen production to Fe(II)-driven nitrate reduction. The equation does not imply quantitative nitrate conversion. The FeOOH plus Mg(OH)2 partition is a speculative mineral-endpoint bookkeeping choice, not a phase identification established by that study; it conserves the idealized mineral inventory while representing Fe(II) oxidation. Nitrate accumulation near 0.1 M, mineral electron capacity and six-week retention are substantial environmental constraints. No molecular O2 is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferroan-brucite reduction of glyoxylate imine to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ferroan brucite surface; additional structural Fe(II) supplies reducing equivalents\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same anoxic alkaline suspension as rxn_014 and in situ condensation rxn_010; six-week nitrate-fed incubation. Allocate mineral reducing capacity to both nitrogen reduction and organic reduction. Measure dissolved glycine separately from mineral-associated glycine. No laboratory mineral digestion is represented as a natural release step, and no complete desorption is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two further Fe(II) centers provide the two-electron reduction of the imine. With M = FeMg2H6O6, the balanced mineral-endpoint model is: HN=CH-COOH + 2 M -> NH2CH2COOH + 2 FeOOH + 4 Mg(OH)2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chimiak et al. (2024) reported approximately 0.1-3.1% total glycine in nitrate-fed experiments after six weeks, versus approximately 52-69% in ammonia-fed systems. In nitrate-fed experiments, 71-97% of detected glycine was mineral-associated, so total formation must not be equated with freely dissolved product. The target node records glycine identity in either inventory; only the measured dissolved fraction is immediately available without release chemistry. As in rxn_014, the exact FeOOH/Mg(OH)2 endpoint is a speculative phase-accounting model, while the overall organic conversion is experimentally supported.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Methane-fed, imine-first Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane/ammonia discharge supplies formaldehyde and cyanide. Transient methanimine captures cyanide, and the resulting aminoacetonitrile passes through glycinamide to glycine during finite hydrothermal processing and cooling. Ammonia and water are boundary resources.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Methane-fed, glycolonitrile-reservoir Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"The same methane-fed precursor source first stores the aldehyde/cyanide inventory as glycolonitrile. Ammoniation feeds aminoacetonitrile formation, followed by the explicitly resolved nitrile and amide hydrolyses. This is a different molecular trajectory from P1, not a claim of an unrelated elementary amination mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-monoxide-fed, imine-first Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Energetic processing of hydrogen-rich CO/N2 supplies fixed nitrogen, formaldehyde and cyanide. Their aqueous reaction follows the methanimine branch, then pressurized hydrolysis and rapid cooling. This branch does not require methane as its carbon feedstock.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-monoxide-fed, glycolonitrile-reservoir Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"CO/N2/H2 discharge products enter a cyanohydrin reservoir before ammonia-dependent conversion to aminoacetonitrile. Subsequent hydrolysis produces free glycine. Product collection, concentration and the hot-zone residence remain explicit requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-dioxide-fed, imine-first Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A sufficiently hydrogen-rich CO2/N2 discharge inventory supplies the precursors for imine-first Strecker synthesis. It is not applicable to an otherwise neutral CO2/N2 atmosphere with little hydrogen. Hydrothermal processing converts the nitrile and amide intermediates to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-dioxide-fed, glycolonitrile-reservoir Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrogen-rich CO2/N2 discharge chemistry is followed by formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation, reservoir ammoniation and two-stage hydrolysis. This pathway differs from P5 by its glycolonitrile intermediate and from P2/P4 by its atmospheric carbon source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Concentrated glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate and buffered ammonium undergo internally balanced hydride transfer, producing glycine together with oxamate. The pathway requires neither cyanide nor an external reductant but begins at a concentrated glyoxylate boundary whose origin is not closed by this network.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-nickel reductive-amination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate and ammonia form a transient imine in situ. Nickel-bearing metal catalyzes its reduction by molecular hydrogen in a cool, pressurized, cyanide-free interface. H2 and reactive Ni are boundary resources; glyoxylate synthesis is not assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced FeS-Fe0 reductive-amination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate and ammonia form the imine, which is reduced by a finite inventory of low-valence iron in an active FeS-Fe0 assemblage. The pathway adopts the demonstrated near-room-temperature organic chemistry without assuming that natural vents automatically generate the laboratory-pretreated material.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrate-fed ferroan-brucite pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Ferroan brucite reduces supplied nitrate to ammonia, enabling in situ condensation with supplied glyoxylate; further mineral reducing equivalents yield glycine. The sequence expresses chemical dependencies within a coupled suspension rather than separate completed batch operations. Dissolved glycine and mineral-associated glycine must be accounted for separately, and no unmodeled acid digestion is credited as natural product release.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "A connected network of 24 chemical-species nodes and 15 reaction nodes defines ten distinct routes to glycine. Six routes combine three experimentally supported atmospheric feedstock inventories with two Strecker entry branches: transient methanimine formation or passage through a glycolonitrile reservoir. These are distinct network pathways, not six independent reaction families. Four cyanide-free routes use glyoxylate through aza-Cannizzaro chemistry, H2/Ni reduction, reduced FeS/Fe0 chemistry, or nitrate/ferroan-brucite chemistry. Glyoxylate supply and concentration are explicit boundary assumptions, not solved upstream synthesis. Atmospheric reactions are lumped precursor-supply transformations rather than elementary reactions or yield predictions. Reaction-specific numerical design assumptions are distinguished from reported experimental conditions. Surface Strecker mixtures undergo advective transfer into moderately hot, pressurized fractures for hydrolysis, followed by cooling; the mineral-reduction branches occupy separate, cyanide-free compartments. Biochemical denotes nonenzymatic mineral-interface chemistry and permits the near-room-temperature conditions actually studied. Neutral organic formulas and explicit proton equivalents provide reference-state bookkeeping; actual protonation, hydration, adsorption and metal speciation depend on pH. environment_formed records one representative setting, not an exclusive location. Mineral resources are finite boundary inventories: neither their natural formation nor their regeneration is assumed. The restricted role vocabulary requires terminal coproducts to be labeled intermediate.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "methanimine", "common_name": "formaldimine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetamide", "common_name": "glycinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C2H3NO2", "iupac_name": "2-iminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylate imine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C2H3NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "oxamic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "zero-valent iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton equivalent", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "NO3-", "iupac_name": "nitrate", "common_name": "nitrate", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "FeMg2H6O6", "iupac_name": "iron(II) dimagnesium hexahydroxide", "common_name": "ferroan brucite, idealized Fe:Mg ratio 1:2", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "ferric oxyhydroxide, modeled oxidation sink", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "MgH2O2", "iupac_name": "magnesium hydroxide", "common_name": "brucite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Methane-ammonia discharge supplies Strecker precursors", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk gas-water system; transient discharge channels are much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 0.7-1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic gas-water interface with repeated electrical discharge and condensation. Johnson's reconstructed volcanic experiment used initial CH4:NH3:H2 partial pressures of 200:200:100 torr and approximately one week of sparking. Bulk temperature here is a design range. Hydrogen may be present initially although it is a product in the formal net equation. Rainout or condensate drainage collects soluble precursors; subsequent concentration requires sheltered, partially evaporating pools rather than a dilute ocean."}, "mechanism": "Electrical fragmentation and radical recombination generate carbonyl and cyanide species, which dissolve in water. Balanced precursor bookkeeping: 2 CH4 + NH3 + H2O -> CH2O + HCN + 5 H2. This equation is a selected-product projection, not an elementary step, fixed product distribution or conversion yield.", "reasoning": "Discharge production of glycine precursors and downstream Strecker chemistry is supported by Miller (1953, 1957), with experimentally productive volcanic apparatus configurations documented by Johnson et al. (2008). A persistent global methane-ammonia atmosphere is not assumed: the plausible boundary is a transient reducing atmosphere or localized reduced plume. Ammonia photolysis, precursor dilution and uncertain gas replenishment limit environmental productivity. No mineral is required for this precursor-generation experiment."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Hydrogen-rich carbon-monoxide-nitrogen discharge supplies Strecker precursors", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk collection system; nonthermal electrical excitation", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Illustrative design boundary: dry-gas H2:CO:N2 ratio 8:1:1, equilibrated with liquid water and repeatedly discharged. This ratio is not presented as an exact published recipe. Operate without O2; trap products in water and concentrate them in partially enclosed shoreline pools. Sustained hydrogen-rich gas supply is required."}, "mechanism": "Discharge activates CO and N2; hydrogen-bearing radicals support formation of HCN, ammonia and formaldehyde. Balanced selected-product net: 2 CO + N2 + 4 H2 -> HCN + CH2O + NH3 + H2O. Water is physically present even though its formal net coefficient is on the product side.", "reasoning": "Schlesinger and Miller (1983) demonstrated strong redox dependence of amino-acid and precursor production in CO-containing atmospheres, with improved HCN and formaldehyde production under sufficiently hydrogen-rich conditions. The equation represents a lumped inventory-producing network, not thermal reduction of N2 by H2 at 300 K. The stated gas ratio is a testable design assumption; its planetary persistence and product collection efficiency are unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrogen-rich carbon-dioxide-nitrogen discharge supplies Strecker precursors", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk collection system; transient energetic discharge", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Illustrative design boundary: dry-gas H2:CO2:N2 ratio 8:1:1 over liquid water, with repeated discharge and aqueous trapping. This is a proposed hydrogen-rich operating inventory, not an exact published recipe. A neutral CO2-N2 atmosphere without substantial H2 is explicitly excluded from the productive scenario. Concentration occurs after collection in shaded or sediment-covered aqueous pockets."}, "mechanism": "Energetic activation, reduction and C-N coupling produce a mixed precursor inventory. Balanced selected-product net: 2 CO2 + N2 + 6 H2 -> HCN + CH2O + NH3 + 3 H2O. The equation does not imply selective or spontaneous ambient-temperature CO2 fixation.", "reasoning": "Schlesinger and Miller (1983) support precursor and glycine production from sufficiently reducing CO2-containing gas systems and show sharply declining productivity as hydrogen availability falls. This branch has a larger reduction demand than the CO branch. Transient hydrogen-rich atmospheric conditions are a boundary assumption, not a settled Hadean atmospheric model, and gas-to-usable-precursor flux remains unquantified."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Reversible formaldehyde ammoniation to methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed concentrated-pool window: pH 8-9.5, approximately 10-100 mM total formaldehyde and 0.1-0.3 M total ammonia plus ammonium. These concentration ranges are design assumptions, not attributed to one experiment. Use covered porewater or shallow sediment to reduce UV destruction. Methanimine is a transient equilibrium species, not an isolated bulk feedstock."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to formaldehyde through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration: CH2O + NH3 <-> CH2NH + H2O. Subsequent cyanide trapping can pull this unfavorable aqueous condensation forward.", "reasoning": "The imine-first Strecker mechanism is consistent with Miller (1957) and the experimentally established aldehyde-ammonia-cyanide chemistry summarized by Hennet et al. (1992). Formaldehyde hydration and imine hydrolysis compete strongly in water, so the node does not assert quantitative methanimine accumulation. Evaporation must concentrate reactants without assuming that volatile HCN and ammonia are retained perfectly."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanide capture of methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same aqueous compartment as rxn_004; proposed pH 8-9.5 and 10-100 mM total cyanide. HCN/CN- and imine/iminium speciation supplies the reactive pair. No Ni, Cu or Fe0 catalyst is introduced into this cyanide-rich branch."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer: CH2NH + HCN -> NH2CH2CN.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile formation as the central link between discharge-generated aldehydes/cyanide and amino acids. The two-carbon glycine skeleton is assembled here without a mineral catalyst. Competing cyanide polymerization, cyanohydrin formation and dilution constrain the fraction entering aminoacetonitrile; the graph does not assign a universal yield."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7.5-9.5 and millimolar-to-0.1 M formaldehyde/cyanide in collected condensate. Partial evaporation or restricted porewater exchange increases concentration. UV-exposed production zones drain into shaded reaction reservoirs."}, "mechanism": "CN- adds reversibly to formaldehyde and the alkoxide is protonated: CH2O + HCN <-> HOCH2CN.", "reasoning": "Formaldehyde-cyanide capture and glycolonitrile participation are supported by the Strecker framework of Miller (1957) and the cyanohydrin-centered chemistry used by Patel et al. (2015). No copper catalyst, sulfide donor or UV photoredox step is required for this particular addition. Glycolonitrile provides an alternative entry reservoir, not evidence for an independently complete cyanosulfidic pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Net ammoniation of the glycolonitrile reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich aqueous reservoir, proposed pH 8-10 and 0.1-0.3 M total ammonia plus ammonium. The actual conversion time and cyanohydrin/aminonitrile distribution require measurement. Retain the mixture in covered pores rather than treating drying to completion as necessary."}, "mechanism": "Lumped reversible cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion: HOCH2CN + NH3 <-> NH2CH2CN + H2O. This can proceed through cyanohydrin dissociation, carbonyl ammoniation and cyanide recapture; it is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) and Patel et al. (2015) support the interconnected cyanohydrin/aminonitrile chemistry underlying this branch. Its distinction from rxn_004-rxn_005 is passage through an accumulated glycolonitrile reservoir, not necessarily a different elementary C-N bond-forming mechanism. Ammonia supply, equilibrium competition and possible cyanohydrin hydrolysis remain limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity providing acid catalysis; no mineral catalyst required"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 2-4 in a moderately hot fracture, not a black-smoker core. Aminonitrile-containing surface water enters coastal subseafloor fractures by advective groundwater exchange. A 6-48 h total hot-zone residence is a screening window shared with rxn_009, not an established conversion time. Unreacted cyanide is not assumed to disappear quantitatively, and this flow path does not enter the Ni/Fe reduction compartments."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile, proton transfers and tautomerization yield the amide: NH2CH2CN + H2O -> NH2CH2CONH2.", "reasoning": "Kamaluddin et al. (1979) observed aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide before amino-acid accumulation. Hennet et al. (1992) demonstrated overall Strecker-compatible glycine synthesis around 423 K and approximately 10 bar. These studies support the chemistry but do not establish isolated-step kinetics for the proposed pH/residence window. Nitrile hydrolysis is therefore retained as a kinetic bottleneck rather than treated as instantaneous."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis and preservation of free glycine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity providing acid catalysis; no mineral catalyst required"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K during hydrolysis; below 330 K after outflow cooling", "pressure": "10-30 bar during hydrolysis", "catalyst": "Aqueous acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 2-4 during the same finite hot-zone residence as rxn_008. Outflow mixes with approximately 5-10 volumes of cooler anoxic water and contacts carbonate-buffered sediments, targeting below 330 K and pH 6-8 within minutes to hours. These are physical design targets, not measured natural residence times. Cooling dilutes the product and does not itself solve subsequent concentration."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the amide carbonyl and C-N cleavage releases glycine and ammonia: NH2CH2CONH2 + H2O -> NH2CH2COOH + NH3. Actual products are protonated or zwitterionic according to pH.", "reasoning": "The glycinamide-to-glycine sequence is supported by Kamaluddin et al. (1979), while Hennet et al. (1992) supports glycine formation during hydrothermal Strecker processing. This is ordinary amide hydrolysis, not release from an unidentified polymer inferred solely after 6 M HCl work-up. Nevertheless, conversion under the proposed milder acidity must be measured. Finite heating and explicit cooling address competing cyclization, oligomerization and decomposition; quantitative survival is not assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Glyoxylate-ammonia condensation to a reducible imine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-303 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar, or 5 bar H2 when coupled to rxn_011", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate cyanide-free mineral-interface compartments. Use the downstream branch's pH: approximately 8 for Ni/H2, 9.6 for reduced FeS/Fe0, or alkaline conditions for ferroan brucite. Initial glyoxylate is millimolar to 0.1 M depending on the cited system. Ammonia may be supplied initially or generated in situ by rxn_014. The imine is formed and consumed in situ, not transferred as an isolated stock solution."}, "mechanism": "Reversible ammonia addition to glyoxylate through a hemiaminal, followed by dehydration: OHC-COOH + NH3 <-> HN=CH-COOH + H2O. Reduction can pull the equilibrium toward product.", "reasoning": "Reductive amination of glyoxylate is supported by Kitadai et al. (2019), Kaur et al. (2024) and Chimiak et al. (2024). The imine is a conventional mechanistic representation, not a claim that every study isolated or quantified it. Glyoxylate hydration and limited imine abundance compete with reduction. The origin and concentration of the supplied C2 substrate remain explicit feedstock-closure gaps."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of glyoxylate imine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Reactive nickel-bearing metallic surface, including the experimentally tested meteoritic analogue"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Nickel-bearing metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "Representative reported regime: approximately 30 mM initial ketoacid, pH 8, ammonium bicarbonate-containing water and 72 h reaction time. The concentration refers to starting glyoxylate equivalents, not isolated imine. A cool, gas-charged subsurface mineral interface can maintain pressure. Exclude cyanide and strongly sulfide-rich inflow to limit metal poisoning. Reactive Ni surface availability is a boundary assumption."}, "mechanism": "Nickel dissociates H2 and transfers reducing equivalents to the C=N bond: HN=CH-COOH + H2 -> NH2CH2COOH.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven ketoacid reductive amination with Ni-containing catalysts and Ni-bearing meteorite material; metal-free controls did not produce the corresponding reduction products. This branch uses H2 as the consumed reductant and Ni as catalyst, unlike the consumable iron systems. The reported 6-84% range spans multiple substrates and catalyst conditions and is not assigned here as a glycine-specific yield. Natural Ni abundance, accessible surface area, poisoning and competing glyoxylate reduction to glycolate limit extrapolation."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Reduction of glyoxylate imine by reduced FeS-Fe0 material", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_019"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Electrochemically pre-reduced FeS-Fe0 assemblage; FeS is the interfacial matrix and Fe0 is explicitly consumed"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in the laboratory analogue", "catalyst": "FeS interfacial matrix; zero-valent iron is a stoichiometric reductant, not a regenerated catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported organic-reaction regime: pH approximately 9.6 and 48 h, with millimolar glyoxylate and excess ammonium. Active material was prepared at potentials at or below approximately -0.7 V versus SHE. That preparation is a boundary condition, not an assumed spontaneous mineral-formation step. Explicit H+ denotes buffered proton equivalents, not addition of strong acid to the alkaline suspension. Fe2+ is reference-state redox bookkeeping and need not accumulate as free aquo ion."}, "mechanism": "Interfacial electron transfer reduces the imine while low-valence iron is oxidized. Balanced reference-state net: HN=CH-COOH + Fe0 + 2 H+ -> NH2CH2COOH + Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) reported approximately 59% glycine under the active reduced-FeS conditions. This supports glyoxylate reductive amination, but the Fe0-to-FeII equation is a simplified electron-source accounting model, not a complete surface mechanism or phase-speciation measurement. FeII can complex, adsorb or precipitate at the stated pH. Natural formation and persistence of comparably active FeS-Fe0 material, its finite electron capacity and passivation remain unresolved; no iron regeneration or universal catalytic turnover is implied."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Buffered glyoxylate aza-Cannizzaro disproportionation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Phosphate buffer supporting proton transfer; no metal catalyst or external reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported regime: approximately 0.5 M glyoxylic acid and 0.5 M ammonium phosphate, pH 7-10, for 48 h. Use a strongly concentrated evaporitic porewater or covered pool margin. Below roughly 0.25 M glyoxylate, little reaction occurred on the studied timescale. Buffer is chemically important; unbuffered glyoxylate plus stoichiometric NH4OH is not treated as equivalent."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate-derived imine/hemiaminal species exchange a hydride: one carbon center is reduced to glycine and the other is oxidized to oxamate. Balanced neutral-reference net: 2 OHC-COOH + 2 NH3 -> NH2CH2COOH + NH2COCOOH + H2O.", "reasoning": "Jarois et al. (2024) reported approximately 11-14% glycine near pH 7-10 under the concentrated, buffered 323 K conditions, with isotopic evidence supporting glyoxylate-derived hydride transfer. This is a supported cyanide-free route without an external redox reagent. Its dominant limitation is achieving and maintaining approximately molar-scale combined solute inventories; ordinary dilute seawater is not a credible substitute. Phosphate functions as part of the proton-transfer environment rather than an assumed generic mineral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Ferroan-brucite-mediated nitrate reduction supplies ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_002", "mol_023", "mol_024", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite surface; structural Fe(II) is a finite electron donor"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 298 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported boundary concentrations are approximately 100 mM nitrate and 100 mM glyoxylate, with incubation for six weeks. Use an anoxic alkaline mineral slurry; pH 9-11 is a proposed operating bracket rather than an exact reported recipe. Reduction occurs alongside organic chemistry in the same cyanide-free suspension, not necessarily as a completed nitrate pretreatment. H+ is a formal buffered proton equivalent, not bulk acidification."}, "mechanism": "Structural Fe(II) supplies the eight electrons needed to reduce nitrate to ammonia. Let M denote the idealized formula FeMg2H6O6. An atom- and charge-balanced mineral-endpoint model is: NO3- + 8 M + H+ -> NH3 + 8 FeOOH + 16 Mg(OH)2 + 3 H2O.", "reasoning": "Chimiak et al. (2024) demonstrated coupled nitrate/glyoxylate chemistry with ferroan brucite and attributed fixed-nitrogen production to Fe(II)-driven nitrate reduction. The equation does not imply quantitative nitrate conversion. The FeOOH plus Mg(OH)2 partition is a speculative mineral-endpoint bookkeeping choice, not a phase identification established by that study; it conserves the idealized mineral inventory while representing Fe(II) oxidation. Nitrate accumulation near 0.1 M, mineral electron capacity and six-week retention are substantial environmental constraints. No molecular O2 is required."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Ferroan-brucite reduction of glyoxylate imine to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_023", "mol_024"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ferroan brucite surface; additional structural Fe(II) supplies reducing equivalents"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 298 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same anoxic alkaline suspension as rxn_014 and in situ condensation rxn_010; six-week nitrate-fed incubation. Allocate mineral reducing capacity to both nitrogen reduction and organic reduction. Measure dissolved glycine separately from mineral-associated glycine. No laboratory mineral digestion is represented as a natural release step, and no complete desorption is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Two further Fe(II) centers provide the two-electron reduction of the imine. With M = FeMg2H6O6, the balanced mineral-endpoint model is: HN=CH-COOH + 2 M -> NH2CH2COOH + 2 FeOOH + 4 Mg(OH)2.", "reasoning": "Chimiak et al. (2024) reported approximately 0.1-3.1% total glycine in nitrate-fed experiments after six weeks, versus approximately 52-69% in ammonia-fed systems. In nitrate-fed experiments, 71-97% of detected glycine was mineral-associated, so total formation must not be equated with freely dissolved product. The target node records glycine identity in either inventory; only the measured dissolved fraction is immediately available without release chemistry. As in rxn_014, the exact FeOOH/Mg(OH)2 endpoint is a speculative phase-accounting model, while the overall organic conversion is experimentally supported."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Methane-fed, imine-first Strecker pathway", "description": "Methane/ammonia discharge supplies formaldehyde and cyanide. Transient methanimine captures cyanide, and the resulting aminoacetonitrile passes through glycinamide to glycine during finite hydrothermal processing and cooling. Ammonia and water are boundary resources.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Methane-fed, glycolonitrile-reservoir Strecker pathway", "description": "The same methane-fed precursor source first stores the aldehyde/cyanide inventory as glycolonitrile. Ammoniation feeds aminoacetonitrile formation, followed by the explicitly resolved nitrile and amide hydrolyses. This is a different molecular trajectory from P1, not a claim of an unrelated elementary amination mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_0