ment\": 0.87,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Hydrogen-rich CO2 discharge chemistry has experimental support in Schlesinger and Miller (1983); this is not an unsupported ambient-temperature reduction of CO2 and N2. The downstream Strecker route is sound. Confidence remains conditional on the unspecified reducing gas inventory, discharge conditions and aqueous capture. The inferred stoichiometric coefficients cannot establish those operating conditions or resolve hydrolysis and glycine-preservation kinetics.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"State the H2/CO2 ratio, gas pressures, discharge exposure and measured or defensibly estimated precursor concentrations.\",\n        \"Specify aminonitrile hydrolysis and cooling conditions, accounting for glycine cyclization, condensation and decomposition during hot residence.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.83\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.79,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.87,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"The CO2-fed reservoir branch is chemically plausible under the hydrogen-rich discharge conditions supported by the supplied research. Glycolonitrile provides a legitimate intermediate reservoir, but its formation does not by itself demonstrate productive ammoniation. As in the other atmospheric pathways, the missing quantitative link is from captured precursor inventories through hydrolysis to preserved free glycine.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify both the atmospheric production conditions and the ammonia concentration, pH and residence time governing reservoir turnover.\",\n        \"Evaluate the complete aqueous processing sequence with dilution and competing reactions included, reporting free glycine separately from hydrolyzable precursor material.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.94,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.81,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.91,\n      \"feedback\": \"The balanced glycine-plus-oxamic-acid transformation closely matches the experimentally supported aza-Cannizzaro chemistry of Jarois et al. (2024), including the proposed internal hydride transfer. The network appropriately identifies glyoxylate supply and concentration as boundary assumptions. Its main deficiency is that 'buffered' and 'concentrated' remain qualitative: the supplied research reports strong concentration dependence and materially different behavior in unbuffered solutions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify buffer identity and concentration, ammonium loading, pH, temperature and reaction time; the reported 0.5 M glyoxylate, 50 degrees C, 48-hour experiments provide a benchmark.\",\n        \"Quantify the assumed concentrated aqueous inventory and competing losses, acknowledging the weak reaction below approximately 0.25 M on the reported timescale.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.8,\n      \"target